1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,137
Chapter 52: Cardiovascular Disorders.

3
00:00:06,137 --> 00:00:37,475
Physiological Considerations In Pregnancy Diagnosis Of Heart Disease General Pregnancy Considerations Heart Failure Surgically Corrected Heart Disease Valvular Heart Disease Congenital Heart Disease Pulmonary Hypertension Cardiomyopathies Infective Endocarditis Arrhythmias Diseases Of The Aorta Ischemic Heart Disease Common Obstetrical Medications References In an analysis of maternal mortality in the United States between 2011 and 2013, deaths related to hemorrhage, hypertensive disorders, and

4
00:00:37,475 --> 00:00:40,262
embolism showed declining rates.

5
00:00:40,262 --> 00:00:54,075
In contrast, deaths attributable to cardiovascular diseases are rising and are responsible for one fourth of all pregnancyrelated mortalities (Creanga, 2017; Petersen, 2019).

6
00:00:54,075 --> 00:01:03,237
Recent development of state-level maternal mortality review committees have emphasized this issue and highlighted the relatively protracted timeline of illness.

7
00:01:03,237 --> 00:01:11,137
Up to a year after delivery, cardiac events were among the leading causes of death for women in Texas in 2013 .

8
00:01:11,137 --> 00:01:18,875
These disorders account also for significant maternal morbidity and are a common reason for intensive care unit admissions .

9
00:01:18,875 --> 00:01:24,987
Cardiovascular disease complicates 1 to 4 percent of pregnancies in the United States .

10
00:01:24,987 --> 00:01:32,962
The increasing prevalence is likely multifactorial and includes the higher rates of obesity, hypertension, and diabetes .

11
00:01:32,962 --> 00:01:42,462
According to the National Center for Health Statistics, almost half of adults aged 20 and older have at least one risk factor for cardiovascular disease .

12
00:01:42,462 --> 00:01:45,887
Another related reason is delayed childbearing.

13
00:01:45,887 --> 00:01:54,975
Last, as discussed subsequently (p. 927), an increasing number of women with congenital heart disease are now becoming pregnant.

14
00:01:54,975 --> 00:02:07,200
The importance of heart disease and its adverse effect on pregnancy morbidity and mortality led the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists to create a Task Force on Pregnancy and Heart Disease.

15
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:23,800
Its purpose is to emphasize the prevalence and effect of heart disease in pregnancy, provide guidance for risk factor identification, outline common cardiovascular disorders, provide recommendations for management, and develop a comprehensive interpregnancy plan.

16
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:26,825
Multidisciplinary care is essential .

17
00:02:26,825 --> 00:02:30,375
Physiological Considerations In Pregnancy.

18
00:02:30,375 --> 00:02:33,775
# Cardiovascular Physiology.

19
00:02:33,775 --> 00:02:43,400
The marked pregnancy-induced anatomical and functional changes in cardiac physiology can have a profound, negative effect on underlying heart disease.

20
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:51,400
These changes are discussed in detail in Chapter 4 (p. 62), and some are listed in Table 52-1 .

21
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:57,425
Importantly, cardiac output increases approximately 40 percent during pregnancy.

22
00:02:57,425 --> 00:03:03,662
Almost half of this total takes place by 8 weeks’ gestation and is maximal by midpregnancy .

23
00:03:03,662 --> 00:03:10,187
This early rise stems from augmented stroke volume, which results from lowered vascular resistance.

24
00:03:10,187 --> 00:03:20,837
Later in pregnancy, resting pulse and stroke volume are even higher because of greater end-diastolic ventricular volume that results from augmented pregnancy blood volume.

25
00:03:20,837 --> 00:03:25,700
These adaptations are even more profound in multifetal pregnancies .

26
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:34,325
Intrinsic left ventricular contractility does not change, and thus normal left ventricular function is maintained during pregnancy.

27
00:03:34,325 --> 00:03:41,200
Namely, pregnancy is not characterized by hyperdynamic function or a high cardiac-output state.

28
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:43,837
Table 52-1.

29
00:03:43,837 --> 00:03:59,337
Hemodynamic Changes in 10 Normal Pregnant Women at Term Compared with Repeat Values Obtained 12 Weeks Postpartum Women with underlying cardiac disease may not always accommodate these changes, and ventricular dysfunction leads to cardiogenic heart failure.

30
00:03:59,337 --> 00:04:06,075
A few women with severe cardiac dysfunction can experience evidence of heart failure before midpregnancy.

31
00:04:06,075 --> 00:04:15,637
In others, heart failure may develop after 28 weeks’ gestation, when pregnancy-induced hypervolemia and cardiac output reach their maximum.

32
00:04:15,637 --> 00:04:25,712
In most, however, heart failure develops peripartum, when labor, delivery, and several common obstetrical conditions add undue cardiac burdens.

33
00:04:25,712 --> 00:04:31,537
Some of the latter include preeclampsia, hemorrhage and anemia, and sepsis.

34
00:04:31,537 --> 00:04:34,937
# Ventricular Function in Pregnancy.

35
00:04:34,937 --> 00:04:38,325
Ventricular volumes and mass accrue to

36
00:04:38,476 --> 00:04:41,913
accommodate pregnancy-induced hypervolemia.

37
00:04:41,863 --> 00:04:46,913
This is reflected by greater end-systolic and end-diastolic dimensions.

38
00:04:46,913 --> 00:04:52,513
At the same time, however, septal thickness and ejection fraction are unchanged.

39
00:04:52,513 --> 00:05:06,063
This is because these alterations are accompanied by substantive ventricular remodeling—plasticity—which is characterized by eccentric expansion of left-ventricular mass that averages 30 to 35 percent near term.

40
00:05:06,063 --> 00:05:11,763
All of these adaptations return to prepregnancy values within a few months postpartum.

41
00:05:11,763 --> 00:05:23,301
Certainly for clinical purposes, ventricular function during pregnancy is normal as estimated by the Braunwald ventricular function graph (Fig. 4-9, p. 63).

42
00:05:23,301 --> 00:05:31,338
Thus, for given filling pressures, cardiac output is appropriate and allows eudynamic cardiac function during pregnancy.

43
00:05:31,338 --> 00:05:37,388
Esoteric changes in pregnancy-related cardiac physiology continue to be clarified.

44
00:05:37,388 --> 00:05:50,838
In nonpregnant subjects with a normal heart who sustain a high-output state, the left ventricle undergoes longitudinal remodeling, and echocardiographic functional indices of its deformation show normal values.

45
00:05:50,838 --> 00:06:00,238
In pregnancy, the ventricle instead undergoes spherical remodeling, and the calculated indices that measure longitudinal deformation are depressed.

46
00:06:00,238 --> 00:06:13,538
Thus, normal nonpregnant indices are likely less accurate when used to assess function in pregnant women because they do not account for the spherical remodeling (Savu, 2012; Stewart, 2016).

47
00:06:13,538 --> 00:06:29,101
Adjusting for these geometrical changes, Melchiorre and coworkers (2016) studied normal cardiac echocardiographic findings in 559 nulliparas at four points during pregnancy and again at 1 year postpartum.

48
00:06:29,101 --> 00:06:39,376
At term, significant chamber diastolic dysfunction was present in 18 percent and impaired myocardial relaxation was evident in 28 percent of the women.

49
00:06:39,376 --> 00:06:47,588
Also, a significant proportion of women demonstrated a drop in stroke volume index and a tendency toward eccentric remodeling.

50
00:06:47,588 --> 00:06:56,888
These findings suggest cardiovascular maladaptation to the expanded volume demands in a substantial proportion of apparently normal pregnancies.

51
00:06:56,888 --> 00:07:05,563
Significant dyspnea at rest was reported by 7.4 percent of the women at term, most of whom had chamber diastolic dysfunction.

52
00:07:05,563 --> 00:07:11,126
Cardiac function and all signs of dyspnea fully recovered at 1 year postpartum.

53
00:07:11,126 --> 00:07:18,576
Cardiac magnetic resonance (MR) imaging increasingly is used to evaluate cardiac structure and function.

54
00:07:18,576 --> 00:07:29,626
Stewart and associates (2016) performed cardiac MR imaging studies in 23 women longitudinally across pregnancy and at 12 weeks postpartum.

55
00:07:29,626 --> 00:07:39,113
Compared with studies performed at 12 to 16 weeks’ gestation, left ventricular mass grew significantly for both normal-weight and overweight women.

56
00:07:39,113 --> 00:07:51,726
The calculated geometrical ratio of left ventricular mass to left ventricular end-diastolic volume demonstrated concentric remodeling throughout gestation, which resolved by 12 weeks’ postpartum.

57
00:07:51,726 --> 00:07:55,201
The right ventricle also undergoes remodeling .

58
00:07:55,201 --> 00:08:02,563
These observations likely mean that pregnancy causes a mixture of eccentric and concentric ventricular remodeling.

59
00:08:02,563 --> 00:08:05,063
Diagnosis Of Heart Disease.

60
00:08:05,063 --> 00:08:14,151
The physiological adaptations of normal pregnancy can induce symptoms and alter clinical findings that may confound the diagnosis of heart disease.

61
00:08:14,151 --> 00:08:29,363
For example, in normal pregnancy, functional systolic heart murmurs are common, respiratory effort is accentuated, edema frequently accrues in lower extremities after midpregnancy, and fatigue and exercise intolerance often develop.

62
00:08:29,363 --> 00:08:39,801
Some systolic flow murmurs can be loud, and normal changes in the various heart sounds depicted in Figure 52-1 may erroneously suggest cardiac disease.

63
00:08:39,801 --> 00:08:47,313
In contrast, clinical findings that are more likely to suggest heart disease are listed in Table 52-2.

64
00:08:47,313 --> 00:08:49,926
Figure 52-1.

65
00:08:49,926 --> 00:08:54,176
Normal cardiac examination findings in the pregnant woman.

66
00:08:54,176 --> 00:09:03,888
S1 = first sound; M1 = mitral first sound; S2 = second sound; P 2 = pulmonary second sound.

67
00:09:03,888 --> 00:09:08,588
Data from Gei, 2001; Hytten, 1991.

68
00:09:08,588 --> 00:09:11,101
Table 52-2.

69
00:09:11,101 --> 00:09:16,613
Clinical Indicators of Heart Disease During Pregnancy # Diagnostic Studies.

70
00:09:16,613 --> 00:09:26,426
Noninvasive cardiovascular studies such as electrocardiography, chest radiography, and echocardiography will provide the data necessary for

71
00:09:26,596 --> 00:09:30,233
cardiac functional evaluation in most women.

72
00:09:30,183 --> 00:09:39,896
In the electrocardiogram (ECG), an average 15-degree left-axis deviation is found as the diaphragm is elevated in advancing pregnancy.

73
00:09:39,896 --> 00:09:43,921
Other findings are described in Figure 52-2 .

74
00:09:43,921 --> 00:09:47,258
Pregnancy does not alter voltage findings.

75
00:09:47,258 --> 00:09:52,233
Atrial and ventricular premature contractions are relatively frequent.

76
00:09:52,233 --> 00:09:54,733
Figure 52-2.

77
00:09:54,733 --> 00:10:09,896
Normal electrocardiograph (ECG) adaptations during pregnancy, including a reduced mean PR interval, increased heart rate, left axis deviation, inverted or flattened T waves, and a Q wave in lead DIII.

78
00:10:09,896 --> 00:10:12,346
HR = heart rate.

79
00:10:12,346 --> 00:10:29,233
Reproduced with permission from Angeli F, Angeli E, Verdecchia P: Electrocardiographic changes in hypertensive disorders of pregnancy, Hypertens Res 2014 Nov;37(11):973–975.

80
00:10:29,233 --> 00:10:40,171
With radiography, anteroposterior (AP) and lateral chest radiographs are useful, and when a lead apron shield is used, fetal radiation exposure is minimal.

81
00:10:40,171 --> 00:10:49,683
Gross cardiomegaly can usually be excluded, but slight heart enlargement is poorly detected because the heart silhouette normally is larger in pregnancy.

82
00:10:49,683 --> 00:10:55,146
This is accentuated further with a portable posterior-anterior chest radiograph.

83
00:10:55,146 --> 00:11:02,358
Echocardiography is now widely used and permits accurate diagnosis of most heart diseases during pregnancy.

84
00:11:02,358 --> 00:11:15,821
Some normal pregnancy-induced changes include a small increase in the dimensions of all cardiac chambers, a slight but significant growth in left ventricular mass, and greater tricuspid and mitral valve regurgitation .

85
00:11:15,821 --> 00:11:21,546
Of note, systolic function normally does not change and ejection fraction is preserved.

86
00:11:21,546 --> 00:11:30,483
Late geometric changes such as interventricular septum dimension (IVd) are likely to be abnormal before changes in ejection fraction.

87
00:11:30,483 --> 00:11:43,383
Savu (2012) and Vitarelli (2011) and their colleagues have provided normal echocardiographic parameters for pregnancy, which are listed in the Appendix (p. 1233).

88
00:11:43,383 --> 00:11:51,296
In some situations, such as complex congenital heart disease, transesophageal echocardiography may be useful.

89
00:11:51,296 --> 00:12:02,433
Cardiovascular MR imaging, compared with echocardiography, is associated with higher reproducibility and is less hindered by ventricular geometry and body habitus.

90
00:12:02,433 --> 00:12:13,721
It is useful for assessment of the right ventricle and visualization of congenital heart lesions and myocarditis (Herrey, 2019; Martin, 2017).

91
00:12:13,721 --> 00:12:20,296
Ducas and coworkers (2014) have published normal reference values for pregnancy.

92
00:12:20,296 --> 00:12:25,808
Exercise stress testing is an objective assessment of maternal functional capacity.

93
00:12:25,808 --> 00:12:30,221
It is also useful to diagnose exercise-induced arrhythmias.

94
00:12:30,221 --> 00:12:39,133
Stress testing can be performed in women with known heart disease prior to pregnancy or, if necessary, in the asymptomatic pregnant women .

95
00:12:39,133 --> 00:12:47,183
Dennis and associates (2019) have described findings in normal pregnant women using the 6-minute walk test.

96
00:12:47,183 --> 00:12:57,733
Myocardial perfusion studies using albumin or red cells tagged with technetium-99m are rarely needed during pregnancy to evaluate ventricular function.

97
00:12:57,733 --> 00:13:05,771
That said, the estimated fetal radiation exposure from nuclear medicine studies of myocardial perfusion is negligible .

98
00:13:05,771 --> 00:13:11,296
Cardiac catheterization with limited fluoroscopy time also is safe to perform.

99
00:13:11,296 --> 00:13:22,196
During coronary angiography, the mean radiation exposure to the unshielded abdomen is 1.5 mGy, and less than 20 percent of this reaches the fetus .

100
00:13:22,196 --> 00:13:31,196
Shortening the fluoroscopic time may help to minimize radiation exposure (Raman, 2015; Tuzcu, 2015).

101
00:13:31,196 --> 00:13:40,821
In women with clear indications, any minimal theoretical fetal risk is outweighed by maternal benefits, and such studies should be performed as indicated.

102
00:13:40,821 --> 00:13:44,571
# Functional Classification of Heart Disease.

103
00:13:44,571 --> 00:13:48,633
Pregnancy is a stress test of cardiovascular reserve.

104
00:13:48,633 --> 00:13:54,808
However, no clinically applicable test accurately measures functional cardiac capacity.

105
00:13:54,808 --> 00:14:14,608
The clinical classification of the New York Heart Association (NYHA) is based on past and present disability and is uninfluenced by physical signs: • Class I. Uncompromised—no limitation of physical activity: These women do not have symptoms of cardiac insufficiency or experience anginal pain.

106
00:14:14,608 --> 00:14:16,346
Class II.

107
00:14:16,346 --> 00:14:22,596
Slight limitation of physical activity: These women are comfortable at rest, but with ordinary

108
00:14:22,756 --> 00:14:30,768
physical activity, discomfort in the form of excessive fatigue, palpitation, dyspnea, or anginal pain results.

109
00:14:30,718 --> 00:14:32,431
Class III.

110
00:14:32,431 --> 00:14:44,256
Marked limitation of physical activity: These women are comfortable at rest, but less than ordinary activity causes excessive fatigue, palpitation, dyspnea, or anginal pain.

111
00:14:44,256 --> 00:14:45,981
Class IV.

112
00:14:45,981 --> 00:14:56,631
Severely compromised—inability to perform any physical activity without discomfort: Symptoms of cardiac insufficiency or angina may develop even at rest.

113
00:14:56,631 --> 00:15:01,393
If any physical activity is undertaken, discomfort is increased.

114
00:15:01,393 --> 00:15:06,918
At least four predictive systems detect and classify heart disease in pregnant women.

115
00:15:06,918 --> 00:15:14,293
These include CARPREG I and II, ZAHARA, and the World Health Organization (WHO) system .

116
00:15:14,293 --> 00:15:23,406
Of these, the most comprehensive risk stratification system is the modified WHO Risk Classification of Cardiovascular Disease and Pregnancy .

117
00:15:23,406 --> 00:15:34,218
This classification system was validated in an international cohort of 2742 pregnant women with heart disease (van Hagen, 2016).

118
00:15:34,218 --> 00:15:39,656
It is especially useful for assessing maternal risk and for preconceptional counseling.

119
00:15:39,656 --> 00:15:53,393
Lu (2015) and Pijuan-Domènech (2015) and their colleagues concluded that the modified WHO classification provides the greatest predictive accuracy for cardiac complications during pregnancy.

120
00:15:53,393 --> 00:15:56,043
Table 52-3.

121
00:15:56,043 --> 00:16:07,056
World Health Organization (WHO) Risk Classification of Cardiovascular Disease and Pregnancy with Management Recommendations General Pregnancy Considerations.

122
00:16:07,056 --> 00:16:10,068
# Preconceptional Counseling.

123
00:16:10,068 --> 00:16:24,318
Women with severe heart disease will benefit immensely from counseling before pregnancy, and they usually are referred for maternal-fetal medicine or cardiology consultation (Clark, 2012; Wolfe, 2019).

124
00:16:24,318 --> 00:16:29,993
Optimizing cardiac function to mitigate complications during pregnancy is the goal.

125
00:16:29,993 --> 00:16:39,431
Maternal mortality rates generally correlate directly with functional classification, however, this relationship may change as pregnancy progresses.

126
00:16:39,431 --> 00:16:51,868
Siu and coworkers (2001b) observed significant worsening of NYHA class in 4.4 percent of 579 pregnancies in which the baseline class was I or II.

127
00:16:51,868 --> 00:17:02,118
As described later, some women have life-threatening cardiac abnormalities that can be reversed by corrective surgery, and subsequent pregnancy becomes less dangerous.

128
00:17:02,118 --> 00:17:09,793
In other cases, such as women with mechanical valves taking warfarin, fetal teratogenic concerns predominate.

129
00:17:09,793 --> 00:17:20,643
Last, because many congenital heart lesions are inherited as polygenic characteristics, some women with congenital heart lesions give birth to similarly affected neonates.

130
00:17:20,643 --> 00:17:25,068
This risk varies widely based on the specific abnormality .

131
00:17:25,068 --> 00:17:27,706
Table 52-4.

132
00:17:27,706 --> 00:18:00,768
Risks for Fetal Heart Lesions Related to Affected Family Members Risk Factors According to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019), four maternal risk factors are linked to cardiovascular disease morbidity and mortality: (1) race/ethnicity, with morbidity highest in African-American women; (2) age, with increased morbidity in women older than 40 years; (3) hypertension of all varieties; (4) obesity, with higher morbidity associated with increasing degrees of obesity .

133
00:18:00,768 --> 00:18:12,556
To these, social and health disparities could be added because lower income, food and housing insecurity, and lack of childcare are directly linked to underutilization of prenatal care .

134
00:18:12,556 --> 00:18:18,531
In turn, lack of prenatal care is a risk factor for maternal morbidity and mortality .

135
00:18:18,531 --> 00:18:21,156
# Antepartum Care.

136
00:18:21,156 --> 00:18:30,793
In most instances, management involves a team approach with an obstetrician, cardiologist, anesthesiologist, and other specialists as needed.

137
00:18:30,793 --> 00:18:39,143
With complex lesions or other high-risk cases, evaluation by a multidisciplinary team is recommended early in pregnancy.

138
00:18:39,143 --> 00:18:47,468
Both prognosis and management are influenced by the type and severity of the specific lesion and by the maternal functional classification.

139
00:18:47,468 --> 00:18:51,456
In some, pregnancy termination may be advisable.

140
00:18:51,456 --> 00:18:59,118
With rare exceptions, women in NYHA class I and most in class II negotiate pregnancy without morbidity.

141
00:18:59,118 --> 00:19:04,906
Special attention is directed toward both prevention and early recognition of heart failure.

142
00:19:04,906 --> 00:19:10,318
Of specific risks, infection with sepsis can precipitate decompensation.

143
00:19:10,318 --> 00:19:12,693
Also, bacterial

144
00:19:12,844 --> 00:19:19,181
endocarditis is a deadly complication of valvular heart disease (p. 934).

145
00:19:19,131 --> 00:19:29,744
Each woman is instructed to avoid contact with persons who have respiratory infections, including the common cold, and to immediately report any evidence for infection.

146
00:19:29,744 --> 00:19:36,844
Pneumococcal vaccine, if not previously administered, and yearly influenza vaccine are recommended .

147
00:19:36,844 --> 00:19:39,644
Cigarette smoking is prohibited.

148
00:19:39,644 --> 00:19:47,556
Illicit drug use may be particularly harmful, an example being the cardiovascular effects of cocaine or amphetamines.

149
00:19:47,556 --> 00:19:52,931
In addition, intravenous drug use raises the infective endocarditis risk.

150
00:19:52,931 --> 00:19:58,644
Fortunately, gravidas in NYHA class III and IV are uncommon today.

151
00:19:58,644 --> 00:20:10,394
In the Canadian study, only 3 percent of the approximately 600 pregnancies were complicated by NYHA class III heart disease, and no women had class IV when first seen .

152
00:20:10,394 --> 00:20:17,444
If a woman chooses pregnancy, she must understand the risks and is encouraged to be compliant with planned care.

153
00:20:17,444 --> 00:20:23,006
In some women, prolonged hospitalization or bed rest is often necessary.

154
00:20:23,006 --> 00:20:25,594
# Labor and Delivery.

155
00:20:25,594 --> 00:20:31,344
In general, vaginal delivery is preferred, and labor induction is usually safe .

156
00:20:31,344 --> 00:20:47,669
From the large Registry on Pregnancy and Cardiac Disease, Ruys and associates (2015) compared pregnancy outcomes between 869 women who had a planned vaginal delivery and 393 gravidas who had a planned cesarean delivery.

157
00:20:47,669 --> 00:20:53,331
Planned cesarean delivery conferred no advantage for maternal or neonatal outcome.

158
00:20:53,331 --> 00:20:57,456
Similar results were reported from Brigham and Women’s Hospital .

159
00:20:57,456 --> 00:21:12,706
Cesarean delivery is usually limited to obstetrical indications, and considerations are given for the specific cardiac lesion, overall maternal condition, and availability of experienced anesthesia personnel and hospital capabilities.

160
00:21:12,706 --> 00:21:22,031
Some of these women tolerate major surgical procedures poorly and are best delivered in a unit experienced with management of complicated cardiac disease.

161
00:21:22,031 --> 00:21:28,394
Occasionally, pulmonary artery catheterization may be needed for hemodynamic monitoring .

162
00:21:28,394 --> 00:21:33,856
In our experiences, however, invasive monitoring is rarely indicated.

163
00:21:33,856 --> 00:22:09,281
Based on her review, Simpson (2012) recommends cesarean delivery for women with the following: (1) dilated aortic root >4 cm or aortic aneurysm; (2) acute severe congestive heart failure; (3) recent myocardial infarction; (4) severe symptomatic aortic stenosis; (5) need for emergency valve replacement immediately after delivery; and (6) warfarin administration within 2 weeks of delivery due to fetal risk for intracerebral hemorrhage because the fetal liver takes up to 2 weeks to metabolize

164
00:22:09,281 --> 00:22:10,894
warfarin.

165
00:22:10,894 --> 00:22:14,994
Although we agree with most of these, we have some caveats.

166
00:22:14,994 --> 00:22:23,669
For congestive heart failure, we prefer aggressive medical stabilization of pulmonary edema followed by vaginal delivery if possible.

167
00:22:23,669 --> 00:22:30,681
During labor, the mother with significant heart disease should be kept in a semirecumbent position with a lateral tilt.

168
00:22:30,681 --> 00:22:34,756
Vital signs are taken frequently between contractions.

169
00:22:34,756 --> 00:22:48,369
Elevations in pulse rate much above 100 beats per minute (bpm) or respiratory rate above 24 breaths per minute, particularly when associated with dyspnea, may suggest impending ventricular failure.

170
00:22:48,369 --> 00:22:55,256
For evidence of cardiac decompensation, intensive medical management must be instituted immediately.

171
00:22:55,256 --> 00:23:02,181
Delivery itself does not necessarily improve the maternal condition and, in fact, may worsen it.

172
00:23:02,181 --> 00:23:07,569
Moreover, emergency cesarean delivery may be particularly hazardous.

173
00:23:07,569 --> 00:23:15,144
Clearly, both maternal and fetal status must be considered in the decision to hasten delivery under these circumstances.

174
00:23:15,144 --> 00:23:20,531
Analgesia and Anesthesia Relief from pain and from apprehension is important.

175
00:23:20,531 --> 00:23:29,881
Although intravenous analgesics provide satisfactory pain relief for some women, continuous epidural analgesia is recommended for most.

176
00:23:29,881 --> 00:23:34,781
The major problem with conduction analgesia is maternal hypotension .

177
00:23:34,781 --> 00:23:40,731
This is especially dangerous in women with intracardiac shunts in whom flow may be reversed.

178
00:23:40,731 --> 00:23:47,794
Hypotension can also be life-threatening if there is pulmonary arterial hypertension or aortic stenosis.

179
00:23:47,794 --> 00:23:52,556
In these, ventricular output is dependent on adequate preload.

180
00:23:52,556 --> 00:23:57,881
In women with these conditions, narcotic regional analgesia; low-dose,

181
00:23:58,036 --> 00:24:02,811
slow-infusion epidural; or general anesthesia may be preferable.

182
00:24:02,761 --> 00:24:12,598
For vaginal delivery in women with only mild cardiovascular compromise, epidural analgesia given with intravenous sedation often suffices.

183
00:24:12,598 --> 00:24:19,836
This minimizes intrapartum cardiac output fluctuations and allows forceps or vacuum-assisted delivery.

184
00:24:19,836 --> 00:24:28,911
Incrementally dosed subarachnoid blockade is approached especially cautiously in women with significant heart disease due to associated hypotension.

185
00:24:28,911 --> 00:24:40,061
For cesarean delivery, epidural analgesia is preferred by most clinicians with caution for its use with pulmonary arterial hypertension (p. 932).

186
00:24:40,061 --> 00:24:49,061
Intrapartum Heart Failure From the Nationwide Inpatient Sample, one fourth of cases of heart failure during pregnancy were encountered intrapartum .

187
00:24:49,061 --> 00:24:53,748
Women with underlying cardiovascular disorders are at higher risk .

188
00:24:53,748 --> 00:25:00,086
The physiological stress and fluid shifts associated with labor and delivery explain this risk.

189
00:25:00,086 --> 00:25:08,211
Catecholamine release due to pain and second stage Valsalva increase left ventricular work, which can precipitate heart failure .

190
00:25:08,211 --> 00:25:13,173
Obstetrical complications can either advance or precipitate heart failure.

191
00:25:13,173 --> 00:25:17,511
Preeclampsia is common and may provoke afterload failure .

192
00:25:17,511 --> 00:25:29,386
Findings from the Registry on Pregnancy and Cardiac Disease indicate that women with preexisting heart disease who develop preeclampsia have a 30percent risk of developing heart failure during pregnancy .

193
00:25:29,386 --> 00:25:32,511
Obesity is another common cofactor .

194
00:25:32,511 --> 00:25:43,136
Also, high-output states caused by hemorrhage and acute anemia elevate cardiac workload and magnify the physiological effects of compromised ventricular function.

195
00:25:43,136 --> 00:25:51,198
Similarly, infection and sepsis increase cardiac output and oxygen utilization and depress myocardial function.

196
00:25:51,198 --> 00:25:59,473
Chronic hypertension with superimposed preeclampsia is the most frequent cause of heart failure in pregnancy in numerous populations.

197
00:25:59,473 --> 00:26:08,111
Many of these women have preexisting concentric left ventricular hypertrophy (Ambia, 2017, 2018).

198
00:26:08,111 --> 00:26:21,523
In some, mild antecedent undiagnosed hypertension causes covert cardiomyopathy, and when superimposed preeclampsia develops, together they may cause otherwise inexplicable peripartum heart failure.

199
00:26:21,523 --> 00:26:29,098
Obesity is frequently comorbid with chronic hypertension, and it also is associated with ventricular hypertrophy .

200
00:26:29,098 --> 00:26:37,573
Cardiovascular decompensation during labor may manifest as pulmonary edema with hypoxia or as hypotension, or both.

201
00:26:37,573 --> 00:26:44,773
The proper therapeutic approach depends on the specific hemodynamic status and the underlying cardiac lesion.

202
00:26:44,773 --> 00:26:57,936
Continuous pulse oximetry and invasive blood pressure monitoring with an arterial line is helpful in some cases In general, pulmonary edema due to fluid overload is often best treated with aggressive diuresis.

203
00:26:57,936 --> 00:27:03,761
If precipitated by tachycardia, heart rate control with βblocking agents is preferred.

204
00:27:03,761 --> 00:27:13,161
However, unless the underlying pathophysiology is understood and the cause of the decompensation is clear, empirical therapy may be hazardous.

205
00:27:13,161 --> 00:27:19,423
Details of heart failure management and specific cardiovascular disorders are discussed below.

206
00:27:19,423 --> 00:27:21,786
# Puerperium.

207
00:27:21,786 --> 00:27:30,573
Women who have shown little or no evidence of cardiac compromise during pregnancy, labor, or delivery may still decompensate postpartum .

208
00:27:30,573 --> 00:27:39,561
Fluid mobilized into the intravascular compartment and reduced peripheral vascular resistance place higher demands on myocardial performance.

209
00:27:39,561 --> 00:27:47,761
Therefore, meticulous care is continued into the puerperium (Sliwa, 2018; Zeeman, 2006).

210
00:27:47,761 --> 00:27:55,673
Postpartum hemorrhage, anemia, infection, and thromboembolism are much more serious complications with heart disease.

211
00:27:55,673 --> 00:28:01,636
Indeed, these factors often act in concert to precipitate postpartum heart failure.

212
00:28:01,636 --> 00:28:11,348
In addition, sepsis and severe preeclampsia cause or worsen pulmonary edema because of endothelial activation and capillary-alveolar leakage.

213
00:28:11,348 --> 00:28:20,823
For example, women with pregnancy-associated hypertensive diseases have a greater risk for readmission for heart failure within 90 days of delivery .

214
00:28:20,823 --> 00:28:33,448
For puerperal tubal sterilization after vaginal delivery, the procedure can be delayed up to several days to ensure that the mother has normalized hemodynamically and that she is afebrile, not anemic, and

215
00:28:33,604 --> 00:28:35,454
ambulatory.

216
00:28:35,404 --> 00:28:44,379
For those desiring future fertility, contraception is crucial (Abarbanell, 2019; Sobhani, 2019).

217
00:28:44,379 --> 00:28:52,154
Detailed contraceptive advice is available in the U.S. Medical Eligibility Criteria for Contraceptive Use guidelines .

218
00:28:52,154 --> 00:28:56,779
These are discussed in Chapter 38 (p. 664).

219
00:28:56,779 --> 00:28:58,529
Heart Failure.

220
00:28:58,529 --> 00:29:06,229
Primary structural or functional cardiac disorders can lead to this clinical syndrome caused by impaired ventricular function.

221
00:29:06,229 --> 00:29:13,079
Mitral stenosis, pulmonary hypertension, and peripartum cardiomyopathy are some examples.

222
00:29:13,079 --> 00:29:18,891
Chronic pressure overload leads to ventricular dilation and impaired function over time.

223
00:29:18,891 --> 00:29:22,566
Associated perinatal mortality rates are high .

224
00:29:22,566 --> 00:29:24,966
# Diagnosis.

225
00:29:24,966 --> 00:29:32,754
Heart failure is best thought of as chronic underlying ventricular myopathy with episodic worsening causing clinical symptoms .

226
00:29:32,754 --> 00:29:39,154
Thus, failure can have a gradual onset or may present as acute “flash” pulmonary edema.

227
00:29:39,154 --> 00:29:46,354
In pregnant women, onset is most likely at the end of the second or beginning of the third trimester and peripartum .

228
00:29:46,354 --> 00:29:57,916
Of symptoms, dyspnea is universal and others are orthopnea, palpitations, substernal chest pain, nocturnal cough, and a sudden decline in the ability to complete usual duties.

229
00:29:57,916 --> 00:30:06,316
Clinical findings include persistent basilar rales, hemoptysis, progressive edema, tachypnea, and tachycardia.

230
00:30:06,316 --> 00:30:18,079
According to Malhamé and associates (2019), serum levels of brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) are variably elevated (Appendix, p. 1231).

231
00:30:18,079 --> 00:30:23,279
Cardiomegaly and pulmonary edema are hallmark radiographic findings.

232
00:30:23,279 --> 00:30:26,804
Acutely, there is usually systolic failure.

233
00:30:26,804 --> 00:30:40,816
Echocardiography may show an ejection fraction <0.45 or a fractional shortening <30 percent, or both, and an end-diastolic dimension >2.7 cm/m2 .

234
00:30:40,816 --> 00:30:47,041
Coincidental diastolic failure also may be found, depending on the underlying cause .

235
00:30:47,041 --> 00:30:55,504
Last, the cause of ventricular dysfunction—such as valvular abnormalities or concentric hypertrophy—may become apparent.

236
00:30:55,504 --> 00:30:57,729
# Management.

237
00:30:57,729 --> 00:31:04,941
Pulmonary edema from heart failure usually responds promptly with diuretic administration to reduce preload.

238
00:31:04,941 --> 00:31:11,491
Recall that furosemide (Lasix) is a potent venodilator in addition to its diuretic action.

239
00:31:11,491 --> 00:31:20,266
Hypertension is common in pregnancy, and afterload reduction is accomplished with hydralazine, nifedipine, or another vasodilator.

240
00:31:20,266 --> 00:31:27,241
Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors are withheld until after delivery because of marked fetal effects .

241
00:31:27,241 --> 00:31:35,904
β-blocking agents lower mortality rates in the setting of heart failure, and carvedilol (Coreg) is commonly used in pregnancy.

242
00:31:35,904 --> 00:31:42,579
Last, digoxin provides inotropic support and is associated with decreased hospitalizations.

243
00:31:42,579 --> 00:31:51,354
With chronic heart failure, the incidence of associated thromboembolism is high, and therefore prophylactic heparin is often recommended.

244
00:31:51,354 --> 00:31:58,329
Left ventricular assist devices are now employed more frequently for acute and chronic heart failure treatment.

245
00:31:58,329 --> 00:32:06,291
A few reports describe their use during pregnancy (Hamdan, 2017; Makdisi, 2017).

246
00:32:06,291 --> 00:32:23,129
Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) was reported to be lifesaving in a woman with fulminating peripartum cardiomyopathy, and it may be used in women with pulmonary hypertension (Meng, 2017; Pacheco, 2018).

247
00:32:23,129 --> 00:32:25,916
Surgically Corrected Heart Disease.

248
00:32:25,916 --> 00:32:31,354
Most clinically significant congenital heart lesions are repaired during childhood.

249
00:32:31,354 --> 00:32:41,529
Those frequently not diagnosed until adulthood include atrial septal defects, pulmonic stenosis, bicuspid aortic valve, and aortic coarctation .

250
00:32:41,529 --> 00:32:46,341
In some cases, the defect is mild and does not require repair.

251
00:32:46,341 --> 00:32:53,516
In others, a significant anomaly is amenable to corrective surgery, performed ideally before pregnancy.

252
00:32:53,516 --> 00:32:58,141
Rarely, surgical corrections are necessary during pregnancy.

253
00:32:58,141 --> 00:33:01,629
# Valve Replacement Before Pregnancy.

254
00:33:01,629 --> 00:33:10,254
Numerous reports describe subsequent pregnancy outcomes in women who have a prosthetic mitral or aortic valve placed before pregnancy.

255
00:33:10,254 --> 00:33:16,379
From one review, the overall estimated maternal mortality rate was 1.2 percent .

256
00:33:16,379 --> 00:33:30,979
Using the Registry of Pregnancy and Cardiac Disease, the maternal mortality rate was 1.4 percent in women with a mechanical heart valve and 1.5 percent in women with a tissue heart valve (van Hagen, 2015).

257
00:33:30,979 --> 00:33:33,091
Compared with the general

258
00:33:33,244 --> 00:33:40,044
maternal mortality rate measured per 100,000 births, this risk is more than 50-fold higher .

259
00:33:39,994 --> 00:33:45,019
The type of valve, either mechanical or bioprosthetic, is paramount.

260
00:33:45,019 --> 00:33:52,031
From the just-described cohort, mechanical heart valve thrombosis complicated 4.7 percent.

261
00:33:52,031 --> 00:34:04,694
Only 58 percent of women with a mechanical heart valve had a pregnancy free of serious adverse events compared with 79 percent of patients with a tissue heart valve (van Hagen, 2015).

262
00:34:04,694 --> 00:34:14,819
A study of 417 women showed that pregnancy loss occurred in 61 and 15 percent with mechanical and bioprosthetic valves, respectively .

263
00:34:14,819 --> 00:34:23,831
Anticoagulation is a requisite with mechanical valves because of thrombosis risks, and its complications are described in the next section.

264
00:34:23,831 --> 00:34:30,681
Thus, pregnancy is undertaken only after serious consideration for women with a mechanical valve.

265
00:34:30,681 --> 00:34:33,419
Table 52-5.

266
00:34:33,419 --> 00:34:48,194
Selected Outcomes in Pregnancies Complicated by HeartValve Replacement Bouhout and associates (2014) reported the outcomes of 27 pregnancies in 14 women who underwent an aortic valve replacement prior to pregnancy.

267
00:34:48,194 --> 00:34:53,544
Seven of the 27 pregnancies occurred in five women with a mechanical valve.

268
00:34:53,544 --> 00:35:04,344
Complications in this group included two embolic myocardial infarctions and one each of miscarriage, postpartum hemorrhage, placental abruption, and preterm birth.

269
00:35:04,344 --> 00:35:08,381
In the bioprosthetic group, there were nine miscarriages.

270
00:35:08,381 --> 00:35:13,956
Adverse maternal and fetal outcomes plague also women with a mechanical mitral valve.

271
00:35:13,956 --> 00:35:24,119
In one report of 28 pregnancies, Vause and colleagues (2017) described severe maternal morbidity and mortality in 57 percent.

272
00:35:24,119 --> 00:35:29,369
A review of pregnancy outcomes in 800 women reported similar results .

273
00:35:29,369 --> 00:35:38,681
Porcine tissue mitral valves are safer during pregnancy, primarily because thrombosis is rare, and anticoagulation is not required .

274
00:35:38,681 --> 00:35:44,556
However, valvular dysfunction with cardiac deterioration poses a serious risk.

275
00:35:44,556 --> 00:35:53,656
Another drawback is that bioprostheses are less durable than mechanical ones, and valve replacement longevity averages 10 to 15 years.

276
00:35:53,656 --> 00:36:01,294
Batra and coworkers (2018) concluded that pregnancy accelerated the risk for subsequent replacement.

277
00:36:01,294 --> 00:36:05,994
Anticoagulation This is critical for women with mechanical valves.

278
00:36:05,994 --> 00:36:14,869
Unfortunately, warfarin is the most effective anticoagulant for preventing maternal thromboembolism but causes harmful fetal effects.

279
00:36:14,869 --> 00:36:26,219
As seen in Table 52-6, anticoagulation with heparin is less hazardous for the fetus, however, the risk of maternal thromboembolic complications is increased .

280
00:36:26,219 --> 00:36:33,594
As a compromise, some use heparin in early pregnancy, and then transition to warfarin in the second trimester.

281
00:36:33,594 --> 00:36:36,244
Table 52-6.

282
00:36:36,244 --> 00:36:49,056
Maternal and Fetal Composite Outcomes in 800 Women with a Mechanical Heart Value Receiving Anticoagulation Warfarin is teratogenic and causes miscarriages, stillbirths, and fetal malformations .

283
00:36:49,056 --> 00:37:00,356
In one study of 71 women given warfarin throughout pregnancy, the rates of miscarriage were 32 percent; stillbirth, 7 percent; and embryopathy, 6 percent .

284
00:37:00,356 --> 00:37:05,606
The risk was highest when the mean daily dose of warfarin exceeded 5 mg.

285
00:37:05,606 --> 00:37:20,294
Similarly, the American College of Cardiology and the American Heart Association estimate that the risk of embryopathy is dose dependent, and the risk is <3 percent if the warfarin dose is ≤5 mg/d .

286
00:37:20,294 --> 00:37:29,756
Women treated with <5 mg/d of warfarin had fetal risks similar to the low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) regimen .

287
00:37:29,756 --> 00:37:36,394
If the dosage is >5 mg/d, the risk of embryopathy exceeds 8 percent.

288
00:37:36,394 --> 00:37:51,419
Anticoagulation for mechanical valves using low-dose unfractionated heparin (UFH) is definitely inadequate and carries a high associated maternal mortality rate (Chan, 2000; Iturbe-Alessio, 1986).

289
00:37:51,419 --> 00:38:03,794
Even full anticoagulation with either UFH or one of the LMWHs is associated with valvular thrombosis (Leyh, 2002, 2003; Rowan, 2001).

290
00:38:03,794 --> 00:38:09,269
However, compliance with dosing and therapeutic monitoring may have contributed .

291
00:38:09,269 --> 00:38:18,444
Therefore, if full anticoagulation with dose-adjusted UFH or LMWH is used, meticulous monitoring is recommended.

292
00:38:18,444 --> 00:38:31,319
The activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) should be at least 2 times control or anti-Xa levels should be 0.8 to 1.2 U/mL at 4 to 6 hours postdose .

293
00:38:31,319 --> 00:38:35,019
Recommendations for Anticoagulation Several

294
00:38:35,188 --> 00:38:43,000
different treatment options exist, although all are principally based on consensus opinion, and none is completely ideal.

295
00:38:42,950 --> 00:38:53,475
Warfarin and LMWH are associated with fewer valve thromboses and therefore favored over subcutaneous UFH (D’Souza, 2017).

296
00:38:53,475 --> 00:39:09,000
In addition, therapeutic subcutaneous UFH dosing is difficult to achieve due to lower peak plasma concentrations in pregnant women, especially with advancing gestation (Barbour, 1995; Brancazio, 1995).

297
00:39:09,000 --> 00:39:19,163
One guideline from the American College of Cardiology and the American Heart Association offers different treatment options based on trimester and baseline warfarin dose .

298
00:39:19,163 --> 00:39:24,700
Figure 52-3 displays a treatment algorithm based on these guidelines.

299
00:39:24,700 --> 00:39:31,150
All recommendations also include aspirin 75 to 100 mg orally daily.

300
00:39:31,150 --> 00:39:33,788
Figure 52-3.

301
00:39:33,788 --> 00:39:38,875
Algorithm for anticoagulation in gravidas with a mechanical heart valve.

302
00:39:38,875 --> 00:39:54,213
INR = international normalized ratio; IV = intravenous; LMWH = low-molecular-weight heparin; aPTT = activated partial thromboplastin time; UFH = unfractionated heparin.

303
00:39:54,213 --> 00:40:00,963
From Elkayam, 2020; Nishimura, 2014; Roeder, 2011.

304
00:40:00,963 --> 00:40:10,175
Vaginal or cesarean delivery is ideally scheduled to allow controlled discontinuation of anticoagulation and partial reversal of its effects.

305
00:40:10,175 --> 00:40:19,138
This also permits administration of regional anesthesia, which requires a degree of coagulation to avoid epidural hematoma formation .

306
00:40:19,138 --> 00:40:30,138
If delivery intervenes while the anticoagulant is still effective, and extensive bleeding is encountered, protamine sulfate is given intravenously to reverse heparin effects.

307
00:40:30,138 --> 00:40:36,338
Patients should be counseled that in these situations regional anesthesia may not safely be possible.

308
00:40:36,338 --> 00:40:45,713
Following vaginal delivery, anticoagulant therapy with warfarin or heparin may be restarted 6 hours later, usually with no problems.

309
00:40:45,713 --> 00:40:52,838
Following cesarean delivery, full anticoagulation is withheld, but the optimal duration is unclear.

310
00:40:52,838 --> 00:41:04,350
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018a) recommends resuming UFH or LMWH 6 to 12 hours after cesarean delivery.

311
00:41:04,350 --> 00:41:12,875
At Parkland Hospital, however, we wait at least 24 hours following a major surgical procedure given inherent bleeding risks.

312
00:41:12,875 --> 00:41:18,288
Following first-trimester dilation and curettage heparin is begun immediately.

313
00:41:18,288 --> 00:41:23,625
Warfarin, LMWH, and UFH are compatible with breastfeeding.

314
00:41:23,625 --> 00:41:29,750
They do not accumulate in breast milk and thus do not induce anticoagulant effects in the newborn .

315
00:41:29,750 --> 00:41:33,338
# Cardiac Surgery During Pregnancy.

316
00:41:33,338 --> 00:41:41,763
Although usually postponed until after delivery, valve replacement or other cardiac surgery during pregnancy may be lifesaving.

317
00:41:41,763 --> 00:41:48,150
Several reviews confirm that such surgery is associated with major maternal and fetal morbidity .

318
00:41:48,150 --> 00:41:57,525
Elassy and associates (2014) described 23 women who underwent urgent open cardiac surgery for severe valve malfunction.

319
00:41:57,525 --> 00:42:05,075
Two women and 10 fetuses—all at a gestational age below 28 weeks—died before hospital discharge.

320
00:42:05,075 --> 00:42:08,300
Only six fetuses were delivered at term.

321
00:42:08,300 --> 00:42:19,538
In a review of 154 women undergoing bypass surgery during pregnancy, the maternal mortality rate was 11 percent, and the fetal loss rate was 33 percent .

322
00:42:19,538 --> 00:42:39,875
To optimize outcomes, Chandrasekhar and coworkers (2009) recommend that surgery be elective when possible, pump flow rate should remain >2.5 L/min/m2, perfusion pressure should exceed 70 mm Hg, and hematocrit should be kept >28 percent.

323
00:42:39,875 --> 00:42:43,563
# Pregnancy after Heart Transplantation.

324
00:42:43,563 --> 00:42:52,375
Many successful pregnancies have followed cardiac transplantation (D’Souza, 2018; Macera, 2018).

325
00:42:52,375 --> 00:43:03,675
Current recommendations from the International Society of Heart and Lung Transplantation do not discourage pregnancy in stable heart transplant recipients who are more than 1 year posttransplant .

326
00:43:03,675 --> 00:43:09,788
Obviously, a highly specialized level of care and multidisciplinary team is necessary.

327
00:43:09,788 --> 00:43:15,525
The transplanted heart appears to respond normally to pregnancy-induced alterations .

328
00:43:15,525 --> 00:43:19,788
Despite this, complications are common during pregnancy.

329
00:43:19,788 --> 00:43:33,325
Of 103 pregnancies in 57 heart recipients from the National Transplantation Pregnancy Registry, almost half developed hypertension, and 11 percent suffered at least one rejection episode during pregnancy .

330
00:43:33,325 --> 00:43:36,913
They were usually delivered by cesarean at a mean

331
00:43:37,084 --> 00:43:39,871
of 37 weeks’ gestation.

332
00:43:39,821 --> 00:43:44,759
Life expectancy following heart transplantation is known to be limited.

333
00:43:44,759 --> 00:43:50,109
At follow-up, at least 16 women had died more than 2 years postpartum.

334
00:43:50,109 --> 00:43:56,046
In another small study, pregnancy itself did not worsen long-term survival rates .

335
00:43:56,046 --> 00:43:58,296
Valvular Heart Disease.

336
00:43:58,296 --> 00:44:09,421
Rheumatic fever is uncommon in the United States because of less crowded living conditions, penicillin availability, and evolution of nonrheumatogenic streptococcal strains.

337
00:44:09,421 --> 00:44:21,534
Still, it remains the chief cause of serious mitral valvular disease in women of childbearing age in the nonindustrialized world (Liaw, 2021; van Hagen, 2018).

338
00:44:21,534 --> 00:44:24,184
# Mitral Stenosis.

339
00:44:24,184 --> 00:44:28,821
Rheumatic endocarditis causes most mitral stenosis lesions.

340
00:44:28,821 --> 00:44:40,171
The normal mitral valve surface area is 4.0 cm2, and when stenosis narrows this to <2.5 cm2, symptoms usually develop.

341
00:44:40,171 --> 00:44:45,284
The contracted valve impedes blood flow from the left atrium to the ventricle.

342
00:44:45,284 --> 00:44:56,959
With more severe stenosis, the left atrium dilates, left atrial pressure is chronically elevated, and significant pulmonary hypertension develops (Galiè, 2016).

343
00:44:56,959 --> 00:45:09,459
These women have a relatively fixed cardiac output, and thus the increased preload of normal pregnancy and other factors that raise cardiac output may cause ventricular failure and pulmonary edema.

344
00:45:09,459 --> 00:45:16,146
Cardiac failure develops for the first time during pregnancy in one fourth of women with mitral stenosis .

345
00:45:16,146 --> 00:45:25,959
The resulting pulmonary venous hypertension and pulmonary edema create symptoms of dyspnea, fatigue, palpitations, cough, and hemoptysis.

346
00:45:25,959 --> 00:45:38,284
The classic murmur may not be heard in some women, and this clinical picture at term may be confused with idiopathic peripartum cardiomyopathy (Cunningham, 1986, 2019).

347
00:45:38,284 --> 00:45:40,921
Table 52-7.

348
00:45:40,921 --> 00:45:50,996
Major Cardiac Valve Disorders Also with significant stenosis, tachycardia shortens ventricular diastolic filling time and elevates the mitral gradient.

349
00:45:50,996 --> 00:45:59,809
This too may lead to pulmonary edema, and therefore, sinus tachycardia is often treated prophylactically with β-blocking agents.

350
00:45:59,809 --> 00:46:07,084
Atrial tachyarrhythmias, including fibrillation, are common in mitral stenosis and are treated aggressively.

351
00:46:07,084 --> 00:46:14,896
Atrial fibrillation predisposes to mural thrombus formation and cerebrovascular embolization that can cause stroke .

352
00:46:14,896 --> 00:46:19,321
Atrial thrombosis can also develop despite a sinus rhythm .

353
00:46:19,321 --> 00:46:26,434
Pregnancy Outcomes In general, complications are directly associated with the degree of valvular stenosis.

354
00:46:26,434 --> 00:46:31,871
Women with a mitral-valve area <2 cm2 are at greatest risk .

355
00:46:31,871 --> 00:46:44,384
In one study of 273 gravidas with mitral stenosis, 43 percent developed heart failure, and almost a fourth of these women required admission (van Hagen, 2018).

356
00:46:44,384 --> 00:46:50,059
For women with severe stenosis, half developed heart failure, and one woman died.

357
00:46:50,059 --> 00:46:56,684
Fetal-growth restriction was more common in women with a mitral valve area <1.0 cm2.

358
00:46:56,684 --> 00:47:01,034
Prognosis also is related to maternal functional capacity.

359
00:47:01,034 --> 00:47:12,884
Among 486 pregnancies complicated by rheumatic heart disease—predominantly mitral stenosis—8 of 10 maternal deaths were in women in NYHA classes III or IV .

360
00:47:12,884 --> 00:47:19,084
Management Limited physical activity is generally recommended in women with mitral stenosis.

361
00:47:19,084 --> 00:47:27,834
If symptoms of pulmonary congestion develop, activity is further reduced, dietary sodium is restricted, and diuretics are given.

362
00:47:27,834 --> 00:47:33,084
Also, βblocker drug therapy slows the ventricular response to activity.

363
00:47:33,084 --> 00:47:42,709
If new-onset atrial fibrillation develops, intravenous verapamil, 5 to 10 mg, is given, or electrocardioversion is performed.

364
00:47:42,709 --> 00:47:50,259
For chronic fibrillation, digoxin, a βblocker, or a calcium-channel blocker can slow ventricular response.

365
00:47:50,259 --> 00:47:58,896
Therapeutic anticoagulation is indicated with persistent fibrillation, left atrial thrombus, and/or a history of embolism .

366
00:47:58,896 --> 00:48:04,646
Surgical intervention is considered for women with symptomatic severe mitral stenosis.

367
00:48:04,646 --> 00:48:15,859
Other candidates are those with mitral-valve area 1.5 to 2.0 cm2 complicated by recurrent systemic embolization or severe pulmonary hypertension.

368
00:48:15,859 --> 00:48:19,996
Balloon valvuloplasty is preferred if the valve is pliable .

369
00:48:19,996 --> 00:48:32,621
In one review of 71 pregnant women with severe mitral stenosis and heart failure who underwent percutaneous valvuloplasty, 98 percent were either NYHA class I or II by the time of

370
00:48:32,788 --> 00:48:34,375
delivery .

371
00:48:34,325 --> 00:48:40,550
At a mean of 44 months, the total event-free maternal survival rate was 54 percent.

372
00:48:40,550 --> 00:48:44,900
However, eight women required another surgical intervention.

373
00:48:44,900 --> 00:48:50,475
All of the 66 newborns who were delivered at term had normal growth and development.

374
00:48:50,475 --> 00:48:56,388
Labor and delivery are particularly stressful for women with symptomatic mitral stenosis.

375
00:48:56,388 --> 00:49:02,675
Pain, exertion, and anxiety cause tachycardia with possible rate-related heart failure.

376
00:49:02,675 --> 00:49:06,138
Epidural analgesia for labor is ideal.

377
00:49:06,138 --> 00:49:11,675
Fluid overload should be avoided, and these women are best managed on the “dry” side.

378
00:49:11,675 --> 00:49:19,650
As shown in Figure 52-4 uterine contractions raise cardiac output by increasing circulating blood volume.

379
00:49:19,650 --> 00:49:26,600
Abrupt expansion in preload may elevate pulmonary capillary wedge pressure and cause pulmonary edema.

380
00:49:26,600 --> 00:49:30,050
Wedge pressures rise immediately postpartum.

381
00:49:30,050 --> 00:49:42,600
One hypothesis for this suggests that the loss of the low-resistance placental circulation couples with venous “autotransfusion” from a now-empty, contracted uterus and from the lower extremities and pelvis .

382
00:49:42,600 --> 00:49:47,350
Thus, pulmonary edema may manifest immediately postpartum.

383
00:49:47,350 --> 00:49:50,013
Figure 52-4.

384
00:49:50,013 --> 00:49:57,650
Mean pulmonary capillary wedge pressure measurements (red graph line) in eight women with mitral valve stenosis.

385
00:49:57,650 --> 00:50:05,313
Shaded yellow and blue boxes are mean (± 1 SD) pressures in nonlaboring normal women at term.

386
00:50:05,313 --> 00:50:07,663
A. First-stage labor.

387
00:50:07,663 --> 00:50:12,250
B. Second-stage labor 15 to 30 minutes before delivery.

388
00:50:12,250 --> 00:50:15,663
C. Postpartum 5 to 15 minutes.

389
00:50:15,663 --> 00:50:18,900
D. Postpartum 4 to 6 hours.

390
00:50:18,900 --> 00:50:22,413
E. Postpartum 18 to 24 hours.

391
00:50:22,413 --> 00:50:26,813
Data from Clark, 1985, 1989.

392
00:50:26,813 --> 00:50:31,300
Most prefer vaginal delivery in women with mitral stenosis.

393
00:50:31,300 --> 00:50:38,138
Elective induction is reasonable so that labor and delivery are attended by a scheduled, experienced team.

394
00:50:38,138 --> 00:50:45,813
With severe stenosis and chronic heart failure, insertion of a pulmonary artery catheter may help guide management.

395
00:50:45,813 --> 00:50:48,713
# Mitral Insufficiency.

396
00:50:48,713 --> 00:50:53,700
A trivial degree of mitral insufficiency is found in most normal patients.

397
00:50:53,700 --> 00:51:01,488
But if mitral valve leaflets align improperly during systole, abnormal degrees of mitral regurgitation can develop.

398
00:51:01,488 --> 00:51:06,975
This is eventually followed by left ventricular dilation and eccentric hypertrophy .

399
00:51:06,975 --> 00:51:16,950
Acute mitral insufficiency is caused by chordae tendineae rupture, papillary muscle infarction, or leaflet perforation from infective endocarditis.

400
00:51:16,950 --> 00:51:31,725
Chronic mitral regurgitation, in contrast, may derive from rheumatic fever, connective tissue diseases, mitral valve prolapse, or left ventricular dilation of any etiology—for example, dilated cardiomyopathy.

401
00:51:31,725 --> 00:51:40,750
Less common causes include a calcified mitral annulus, possibly some appetite suppressants, and in older women, ischemic heart disease.

402
00:51:40,750 --> 00:51:49,163
Mitral valve vegetations—Libman-Sacks endocarditis—are relatively common in women with antiphospholipid antibodies .

403
00:51:49,163 --> 00:51:53,663
These sometimes coexist with systemic lupus erythematosus .

404
00:51:53,663 --> 00:52:03,900
In nonpregnant patients, symptoms from mitral valve insufficiency are rare, and valve replacement is seldom indicated unless infective endocarditis develops.

405
00:52:03,900 --> 00:52:13,688
During pregnancy, mitral regurgitation is similarly well tolerated, probably because the lowered systemic vascular resistance yields less regurgitation.

406
00:52:13,688 --> 00:52:25,825
In the report by van Hagen and associates (2018), of 117 women with moderate or severe regurgitation, 23 percent developed heart failure during pregnancy.

407
00:52:25,825 --> 00:52:31,700
Occasionally tachyarrhythmias or severely depressed systolic function require treatment.

408
00:52:31,700 --> 00:52:34,650
# Mitral Valve Prolapse.

409
00:52:34,650 --> 00:52:47,000
This diagnosis implies the presence of a pathological connective tissue disorder—often termed myxomatous degeneration—which may involve the valve leaflets, the annulus, or the chordae tendineae.

410
00:52:47,000 --> 00:52:49,975
Mitral insufficiency may develop.

411
00:52:49,975 --> 00:52:57,863
Most women with mitral valve prolapse are asymptomatic and are diagnosed during routine examination or echocardiography.

412
00:52:57,863 --> 00:53:06,075
The few women with symptoms have anxiety, palpitations, atypical chest pain, dyspnea with exertion, and syncope .

413
00:53:06,075 --> 00:53:11,475
Pregnant women with mitral valve prolapse rarely have cardiac complications.

414
00:53:11,475 --> 00:53:24,163
Hypervolemia may even improve alignment of the mitral valve, and women without pathological myxomatous degeneration generally have excellent pregnancy outcomes (Leśniak-Sobelga, 2004).

415
00:53:24,163 --> 00:53:26,750
For women who are symptomatic,

416
00:53:26,908 --> 00:53:35,483
β-blocking drugs diminish sympathetic tone, relieve chest pain and palpitations, and reduce the risk of life-threatening arrhythmias.

417
00:53:35,433 --> 00:53:38,120
# Aortic Stenosis.

418
00:53:38,120 --> 00:53:48,283
Since the decline in incidence of rheumatic disease, congenital bicuspid valve is now the most frequent cause of aortic stenosis in young women in the United States .

419
00:53:48,283 --> 00:53:56,758
A normal aortic valve has an area of 3 to 4 cm2, with a pressure gradient <5 mm Hg.

420
00:53:56,758 --> 00:54:06,145
If the valve area is <1 cm2, flow is severely obstructed, and progressive pressure overload on the left ventricle develops.

421
00:54:06,145 --> 00:54:17,670
Concentric left ventricular hypertrophy follows, and if it is severe, end-diastolic pressures become elevated, ejection fraction decreases, and cardiac output is reduced .

422
00:54:17,670 --> 00:54:22,908
Severity is estimated by peak velocity of flow across the aortic valve.

423
00:54:22,908 --> 00:54:43,708
Mild stenosis has a peak velocity of 2–2.9 m/s, moderate 3-3.9 m/s, and severe ≥4 m/s. Corresponding mean pressure gradients are <20, 20–39, and ≥40 mm Hg, respectively .

424
00:54:43,708 --> 00:54:52,045
Characteristic manifestations develop late and include chest pain, syncope, heart failure, and sudden death from arrhythmias.

425
00:54:52,045 --> 00:55:00,495
In asymptomatic patients, the mortality rate is 1 percent per year, but with symptoms it increases to 25 percent per year.

426
00:55:00,495 --> 00:55:05,145
Thus, valve replacement is indicated for symptomatic patients.

427
00:55:05,145 --> 00:55:15,370
Pregnancy In one single-center study, aortic stenosis accounted for 15 percent of cases of congenital heart disease in 178 pregnant women .

428
00:55:15,370 --> 00:55:21,133
That said, clinically significant aortic stenosis is infrequent during pregnancy.

429
00:55:21,133 --> 00:55:25,370
Mild to moderate degrees of stenosis are well tolerated.

430
00:55:25,370 --> 00:55:30,420
Severe disease is lifethreatening and carries a 6-percent mortality risk .

431
00:55:30,420 --> 00:55:37,608
The principal underlying hemodynamic problem is the fixed cardiac output associated with severe stenosis.

432
00:55:37,608 --> 00:55:45,408
During pregnancy, several common events acutely lower preload further and thus aggravate the fixed cardiac output.

433
00:55:45,408 --> 00:55:52,708
These include vena caval occlusion from the gravid uterus, regional analgesia, sepsis, and hemorrhage.

434
00:55:52,708 --> 00:56:01,895
From the earlier-cited Canadian study, complication rates were higher if the aortic valve area measured <1.5 cm2 .

435
00:56:01,895 --> 00:56:09,158
Management For asymptomatic women with aortic stenosis, no treatment except close observation is required.

436
00:56:09,158 --> 00:56:18,958
Management of a symptomatic woman is undertaken in conjunction with a cardiologist and includes strict limitation of activity and cautious use of diuretics.

437
00:56:18,958 --> 00:56:24,820
If symptoms persist, surgical intervention or preterm delivery may be considered.

438
00:56:24,820 --> 00:56:32,383
Catheter-based valvuloplasty is associated with risks to both the mother and fetus and shows poor long-term efficacy .

439
00:56:32,383 --> 00:56:38,495
Namely, the aortic valve can again narrow or new aortic regurgitation may develop.

440
00:56:38,495 --> 00:56:48,958
The alternative surgical approach—valve replacement—is associated with significant risk of fetal demise due to the hypotension associated with cardiac bypass.

441
00:56:48,958 --> 00:57:03,095
Transcatheter aortic valve replacement (TAVR) has been performed in low-risk nonpregnant women, but there is little experience with this procedure during pregnancy (Hodson, 2016; Mack, 2019).

442
00:57:03,095 --> 00:57:21,645
Accordingly, the American College of Cardiology, the American Heart Association, and the European Society of Cardiology recommend delaying conception until after surgical correction for severe aortic stenosis (Nishimura, 2014; Regitz-Zagrosek, 2018).

443
00:57:21,645 --> 00:57:28,333
For those with uncorrected severe symptomatic aortic stenosis, cesarean delivery is preferred.

444
00:57:28,333 --> 00:57:33,595
For asymptomatic women with severe stenosis, care is individualized.

445
00:57:33,595 --> 00:57:37,970
In nonsevere stenosis, vaginal delivery is preferred .

446
00:57:37,970 --> 00:57:44,033
For women with critical aortic stenosis, intensive monitoring during labor is essential.

447
00:57:44,033 --> 00:57:51,770
Pulmonary artery catheterization may be helpful because of the narrow margin separating fluid overload from hypovolemia.

448
00:57:51,770 --> 00:58:01,220
Women with aortic stenosis are dependent on adequate end-diastolic ventricular filling pressures to maintain cardiac output and systemic perfusion.

449
00:58:01,220 --> 00:58:09,608
Abrupt drops in end-diastolic volume may result in hypotension, syncope, myocardial infarction, and sudden death.

450
00:58:09,608 --> 00:58:15,795
Thus, avoiding diminished ventricular preload and maintaining cardiac output are key.

451
00:58:15,795 --> 00:58:21,045
During labor and delivery, affected women are best managed on the “wet” side.

452
00:58:21,045 --> 00:58:22,508
This

453
00:58:22,660 --> 00:58:28,685
provides a margin of safety in intravascular volume in anticipation of possible hemorrhage.

454
00:58:28,635 --> 00:58:33,572
In women with a competent mitral valve, pulmonary edema is rare.

455
00:58:33,572 --> 00:58:42,572
During labor, narcotic or low-dose, slow-infusion epidural analgesia seems ideal and avoids potentially hazardous hypotension.

456
00:58:42,572 --> 00:58:55,347
Easterling and coworkers (1988) studied the effects of epidural analgesia in five women with severe stenosis and demonstrated immediate and profound effects from decreased filling pressures.

457
00:58:55,347 --> 00:59:04,060
Xia and colleagues (2006) emphasize slow administration of dilute local anesthetic agents into the epidural space.

458
00:59:04,060 --> 00:59:12,010
In hemodynamically stable women, forceps or vacuum delivery is used only for standard obstetrical indications.

459
00:59:12,010 --> 00:59:20,247
In those experiencing dizziness, shortness of breath, or tachycardia with pushing, an operative vaginal delivery is preferred.

460
00:59:20,247 --> 00:59:23,110
# Aortic Insufficiency.

461
00:59:23,110 --> 00:59:30,885
Aortic valve regurgitation or insufficiency allows diastolic flow of blood from the aorta back into the left ventricle.

462
00:59:30,885 --> 00:59:38,422
Frequent causes of insufficiency are rheumatic fever, connective tissue abnormalities, and congenital lesions .

463
00:59:38,422 --> 00:59:45,497
With Marfan syndrome, the aortic root may dilate and create regurgitation (p. 936).

464
00:59:45,497 --> 00:59:51,722
Acute insufficiency may also develop with bacterial endocarditis or aortic dissection.

465
00:59:51,722 --> 01:00:07,972
Last, aortic and mitral valve insufficiency have both been linked to the appetite suppressants fenfluramine and dexfenfluramine and to the ergot-derived dopamine agonists cabergoline and pergolide (Schade, 2007; Zanettini, 2007).

466
01:00:07,972 --> 01:00:13,697
With chronic insufficiency, left ventricular hypertrophy and dilation develop.

467
01:00:13,697 --> 01:00:21,947
Slow-onset fatigue, dyspnea, and pulmonary edema follows ventricular dilation with subsequent rapid deterioration .

468
01:00:21,947 --> 01:00:26,647
Aortic insufficiency is generally well tolerated during pregnancy.

469
01:00:26,647 --> 01:00:33,610
Like mitral valve insufficiency, reduced vascular resistance is thought to improve hemodynamic function.

470
01:00:33,610 --> 01:00:39,522
If symptoms of heart failure develop, diuretics are given, and bed rest is encouraged.

471
01:00:39,522 --> 01:00:42,322
# Pulmonic Stenosis.

472
01:00:42,322 --> 01:00:48,722
This lesion is usually congenital and may be associated with Fallot tetralogy or Noonan syndrome .

473
01:00:48,722 --> 01:00:57,497
The greater hemodynamic burden of pregnancy can precipitate right-sided heart failure or atrial arrhythmias in women with severe stenosis.

474
01:00:57,497 --> 01:01:09,985
Surgical correction ideally is done before pregnancy, but if symptoms progress, a balloon valvuloplasty may be necessary antepartum (Galal, 2015; Siu, 2001a).

475
01:01:09,985 --> 01:01:22,510
In studying pregnancy outcomes, Drenthen and associates (2006) found infrequent cardiac complications in 81 pregnancies in 51 Dutch women with pulmonic stenosis.

476
01:01:22,510 --> 01:01:29,722
The NYHA classification worsened in two women, and nine experienced palpitations or arrhythmias.

477
01:01:29,722 --> 01:01:35,610
No changes in pulmonary valvular function or other adverse cardiac events were reported.

478
01:01:35,610 --> 01:01:47,072
However, noncardiac complication rates were significant—17 percent had preterm delivery, 15 percent had hypertension, and 4 percent developed thromboembolism.

479
01:01:47,072 --> 01:01:49,410
Congenital Heart Disease.

480
01:01:49,410 --> 01:01:59,097
The incidence of congenital heart disease in the United States approximates 1.9 percent of live births, and half of these are moderate to severe forms .

481
01:01:59,097 --> 01:02:11,097
With modern surgeries, approximately 90 percent of those born with congenital heart disease survive to childbearing age, and it is now the most common type of heart disease encountered during pregnancy .

482
01:02:11,097 --> 01:02:27,360
Specifically, analysis from the United States Nationwide Inpatient Sample database showed a linear rise in the prevalence of congenital heart disease between 2000 and 2010—from 6.4 to 9.0 per 10,000 women admitted for delivery .

483
01:02:27,360 --> 01:02:36,385
The odds of obstetrical and perinatal complications are increased two- to threefold in women with congenital heart disease compared with unaffected women .

484
01:02:36,385 --> 01:02:49,297
Also, the maternal mortality rate was higher for women with congenital heart disease at 178 per 100,000 deliveries compared with 7 per 100,000 deliveries in unaffected gravidas .

485
01:02:49,297 --> 01:02:52,372
# Atrial Septal Defects.

486
01:02:52,372 --> 01:03:02,972
A patent foramen ovale (PFO) is a persisting incompetence of the fossa ovale, and this flap has the potential to open under increased hydrostatic pressure.

487
01:03:02,972 --> 01:03:07,272
Of all adults, approximately one fourth have this defect .

488
01:03:07,272 --> 01:03:10,522
The small risk of PFO-related stroke,

489
01:03:10,684 --> 01:03:19,684
discussed subsequently, is likely higher in pregnant than in nonpregnant women and mainly attributed to normal pregnancy hypercoagulability .

490
01:03:19,634 --> 01:03:23,109
Preventive PFO repair is not recommended .

491
01:03:23,109 --> 01:03:27,959
An atrial septal defect (ASD) is a true hole in the septum.

492
01:03:27,959 --> 01:03:36,684
The secundumtype defect accounts for 70 percent, and associated mitral valve myxomatous abnormalities with prolapse are common.

493
01:03:36,684 --> 01:03:42,196
Most ASDs are typically asymptomatic until the third or fourth decade of life .

494
01:03:42,196 --> 01:03:46,871
If an ASD is discovered in adulthood, most recommend repair.

495
01:03:46,871 --> 01:03:54,934
Pregnancy with an unrepaired ASD is well tolerated unless pulmonary hypertension has developed, but this is uncommon .

496
01:03:54,934 --> 01:04:00,796
Medical treatment during pregnancy is indicated for congestive heart failure or an arrhythmia.

497
01:04:00,796 --> 01:04:05,046
The risk of endocarditis with an ASD is negligible.

498
01:04:05,046 --> 01:04:10,746
With the potential to shunt blood from right to left, a paradoxical embolism is possible.

499
01:04:10,746 --> 01:04:17,934
A venous thrombus enters the systemic arterial circulation through the ASD, causing an embolic stroke .

500
01:04:17,934 --> 01:04:22,871
For this reason, filters should be placed on intravenous access sites.

501
01:04:22,871 --> 01:04:34,896
In a gravida with an ASD but without current venous thromboembolism (VTE), the decision to add anticoagulant prophylaxis to counter this potential embolism risk is problematic.

502
01:04:34,896 --> 01:04:45,821
However, for a pregnant woman with an ASD who is immobile or has another risk factor for thromboembolism, compression stockings and prophylactic heparin are reasonable.

503
01:04:45,821 --> 01:04:49,059
# Ventricular Septal Defects.

504
01:04:49,059 --> 01:04:54,534
These lesions close spontaneously during childhood in 90 percent of cases.

505
01:04:54,534 --> 01:05:01,359
Of the four main ventricular septal defect (VSD) types, most defects are paramembranous.

506
01:05:01,359 --> 01:05:06,884
This location is well below the outlet valves yet above the ventricular musculature.

507
01:05:06,884 --> 01:05:13,609
The degree of associated left-to-right shunt and physiological derangements are related to lesion size.

508
01:05:13,609 --> 01:05:22,759
In general, if the defect measures <1.25 cm2, pulmonary hypertension and heart failure do not develop.

509
01:05:22,759 --> 01:05:29,334
If the effective defect size exceeds that of the aortic valve orifice, symptoms rapidly develop.

510
01:05:29,334 --> 01:05:36,034
For these reasons, most children undergo surgical repair before pulmonary hypertension develops.

511
01:05:36,034 --> 01:05:45,771
Adults with unrepaired large defects develop left ventricular failure and pulmonary hypertension and have a high incidence of bacterial endocarditis .

512
01:05:45,771 --> 01:05:50,846
Pregnancy is well tolerated with small- to moderate-sized VSD shunts.

513
01:05:50,846 --> 01:06:00,796
However, if pulmonary arterial pressures reach systemic levels, flow is reversed or bidirectional—Eisenmenger syndrome (p. 929).

514
01:06:00,796 --> 01:06:10,209
If this cyanotic condition develops, the maternal and fetal mortality rates are significantly higher, and thus pregnancy is generally not advisable .

515
01:06:10,209 --> 01:06:20,059
Bacterial endocarditis is more common with unrepaired defects, and antimicrobial prophylaxis is often required (p. 934).

516
01:06:20,059 --> 01:06:27,421
As shown in Table 52-4, 16 percent of offspring born to these women also have a VSD.

517
01:06:27,421 --> 01:06:31,121
# Atrioventricular Septal Defects.

518
01:06:31,121 --> 01:06:38,296
An atrioventricular (AV) septal defect is characterized by a common, ovoid AV junction.

519
01:06:38,296 --> 01:06:47,359
These account for approximately 3 percent of all congenital cardiac malformations and are distinct from isolated ASDs or VSDs.

520
01:06:47,359 --> 01:06:53,759
This defect is associated with aneuploidy, Eisenmenger syndrome, and other malformations .

521
01:06:53,759 --> 01:06:59,496
Compared with simple septal defects, complications are more frequent during pregnancy.

522
01:06:59,496 --> 01:07:14,059
In a review of 48 pregnancies in 29 affected women, complications included persistent deterioration of NYHA class in 23 percent, significant arrhythmias in 19 percent, and heart failure in 2 percent .

523
01:07:14,059 --> 01:07:18,959
Congenital heart disease was identified in 15 percent of the offspring.

524
01:07:18,959 --> 01:07:23,271
# Persistent (Patent) Ductus Arteriosus.

525
01:07:23,271 --> 01:07:30,921
The ductus connects the proximal left pulmonary artery to the descending aorta just distal to the left subclavian artery.

526
01:07:30,921 --> 01:07:36,496
Functional closure of the ductus from vasoconstriction occurs shortly after term birth.

527
01:07:36,496 --> 01:07:41,784
The physiological consequences with its persistence are related to its size.

528
01:07:41,784 --> 01:07:45,571
Most significant lesions are repaired in childhood.

529
01:07:45,571 --> 01:07:58,896
However, in women with an unrepaired ductus, pulmonary hypertension, heart failure, or cyanosis will develop if systemic blood pressure falls and blood flow reverses from the pulmonary artery into the aorta .

530
01:07:58,896 --> 01:08:00,721
A sudden blood

531
01:08:00,892 --> 01:08:07,954
pressure decline at delivery—such as with regional analgesia or hemorrhage—may lead to fatal collapse.

532
01:08:07,904 --> 01:08:13,142
Therefore, hypotension is avoided but treated vigorously if it develops.

533
01:08:13,142 --> 01:08:21,092
Prophylaxis for bacterial endocarditis is indicated at delivery for unrepaired defects (p. 934).

534
01:08:21,092 --> 01:08:27,554
As shown in Table 52-4, the incidence of inheritance approximates 4 percent.

535
01:08:27,554 --> 01:08:30,429
# Cyanotic Heart Disease.

536
01:08:30,429 --> 01:08:38,167
Cyanosis develops when congenital heart lesions produce right-to-left shunting of blood past the pulmonary capillary bed.

537
01:08:38,167 --> 01:08:44,679
The classic and most commonly encountered lesion in adults and during pregnancy is the Fallot tetralogy .

538
01:08:44,679 --> 01:08:56,267
This is characterized by a large VSD, pulmonary stenosis, right ventricular hypertrophy, and an overriding aorta that receives blood from both the right and left ventricles.

539
01:08:56,267 --> 01:09:01,504
The magnitude of the shunt varies inversely with systemic vascular resistance.

540
01:09:01,504 --> 01:09:09,029
Hence, during pregnancy, when peripheral resistance decreases, shunt flow increases and cyanosis worsens.

541
01:09:09,029 --> 01:09:13,992
Generally, women with cyanotic heart disease do poorly during pregnancy.

542
01:09:13,992 --> 01:09:19,879
Those with concomitant Eisenmenger syndrome are at greatest risk (p. 929).

543
01:09:19,879 --> 01:09:25,592
With uncorrected Fallot tetralogy, maternal mortality rates approach 10 percent.

544
01:09:25,592 --> 01:09:35,779
For fetal outcome, there is a relationship between chronic hypoxemia, polycythemia, and complications such as miscarriage and perinatal morbidity.

545
01:09:35,779 --> 01:09:46,679
When hypoxemia is intense enough to stimulate a rise in hemoglobin concentration >20 g/dL, pregnancy wastage is virtually 100 percent .

546
01:09:46,679 --> 01:09:56,204
Although not all cyanotic lesions are repairable, with satisfactory surgical correction before pregnancy, maternal and fetal outcomes are much improved.

547
01:09:56,204 --> 01:10:07,054
In a review of 197 pregnancies in 99 women with surgically corrected Fallot tetralogy, pregnancy was usually well tolerated, and no mothers died .

548
01:10:07,054 --> 01:10:20,092
Still, almost 9 percent of pregnancies were complicated by adverse cardiac events including new-onset or worsening arrhythmias and heart failure (Balci, 2011; Kamiya, 2012).

549
01:10:20,092 --> 01:10:28,679
Some women with Ebstein anomaly, characterized by a malpositioned and malformed tricuspid valve, may reach reproductive age.

550
01:10:28,679 --> 01:10:33,317
The right ventricle is small and the right atrium is severely dilated.

551
01:10:33,317 --> 01:10:36,179
Right-sided heart failure is common.

552
01:10:36,179 --> 01:10:43,929
These women are very preload dependent, and pregnancy-induced hypervolemia can worsen the tricuspid regurgitation .

553
01:10:43,929 --> 01:10:50,542
Arrhythmias also are common, especially Wolff-ParkinsonWhite syndrome (p. 936).

554
01:10:50,542 --> 01:10:54,379
Vaginal delivery seems preferable in most cases.

555
01:10:54,379 --> 01:11:02,592
In the absence of cyanosis, heart failure, or significant arrhythmias, affected women usually tolerate pregnancy well .

556
01:11:02,592 --> 01:11:06,029
# Pregnancy after Surgical Repair.

557
01:11:06,029 --> 01:11:14,229
Transposition of the Great Vessels Pregnancy following arterial switch operation for transposition is associated with good outcomes.

558
01:11:14,229 --> 01:11:19,817
A major concern is fatal arrhythmias that usually are precipitated by exercise .

559
01:11:19,817 --> 01:11:28,504
Earlier studies cited a relatively high rate of heart failure and arrhythmias, but more recent studies have favorable pregnancy outcomes .

560
01:11:28,504 --> 01:11:33,504
In one study of 20 pregnancies, there were three cases of heart failure .

561
01:11:33,504 --> 01:11:40,879
Stoll and coworkers (2018) reported no adverse cardiac events in 25 pregnancies.

562
01:11:40,879 --> 01:11:54,504
Of other defects, repaired truncus arteriosus and double-outlet right ventricle with subsequent successful, although eventful, pregnancies have been described (Drenthen, 2008; Hoendermis, 2008).

563
01:11:54,504 --> 01:12:00,317
Ironically, preconceptional counseling did little to dissuade these women from childbearing .

564
01:12:00,317 --> 01:12:08,479
Single Functional Ventricle With hypoplastic left heart syndrome, most affected women are now expected to survive into adulthood .

565
01:12:08,479 --> 01:12:17,329
Frequently, these women become pregnant, and those who have undergone a Fontan repair carry a particularly higher risk for complications.

566
01:12:17,329 --> 01:12:27,079
In brief, this procedure involves diverting blood via a surgical anastomosis from the vena cava to the pulmonary artery without passing through the right ventricle.

567
01:12:27,079 --> 01:12:30,904
Blood flows passively to the pulmonary vasculature.

568
01:12:30,904 --> 01:12:37,742
Preload drives circulation in the Fontan circuit, and thus patients are sensitive to volume changes .

569
01:12:37,742 --> 01:12:49,942
From their review of 255 pregnancies in 133 women, Garcia-Ropero and colleagues (2018) reported 115 miscarriages (45

570
01:12:50,092 --> 01:12:54,529
percent) and 19 elective terminations (7 percent).

571
01:12:54,479 --> 01:13:01,292
Cardiac complications included arrhythmias in 8 percent and heart failure in 4 percent of pregnancies.

572
01:13:01,292 --> 01:13:10,492
Among 133 live births, there were 68 preterm births (59 percent) and 7 perinatal deaths (6 percent).

573
01:13:10,492 --> 01:13:14,379
Postpartum venous thromboembolism also is common .

574
01:13:14,379 --> 01:13:24,379
Similar complications attend a maternal systemic right ventricle, that is, one in which the right ventricle rather than the left pumps blood to the systemic circulation .

575
01:13:24,379 --> 01:13:27,067
# Eisenmenger Syndrome.

576
01:13:27,067 --> 01:13:32,842
This describes secondary pulmonary hypertension that arises from any cardiac lesion.

577
01:13:32,842 --> 01:13:39,879
The most common underlying defects are ASD, VSD, and persistent ductus arteriosus .

578
01:13:39,879 --> 01:13:48,079
The syndrome develops when pulmonary vascular resistance exceeds systemic resistance and leads to concomitant right-to-left shunting.

579
01:13:48,079 --> 01:13:55,817
Patients are asymptomatic for years, but eventually pulmonary hypertension becomes severe enough to cause this shunting.

580
01:13:55,817 --> 01:13:58,542
Figure 52-5.

581
01:13:58,542 --> 01:14:03,529
Eisenmenger syndrome due to a ventricular septal defect (VSD).

582
01:14:03,529 --> 01:14:11,917
A. Substantial left-toright shunting through the VSD leads to morphological changes in the smaller pulmonary arteries and arterioles.

583
01:14:11,917 --> 01:14:20,804
Specifically, medial hypertrophy, intimal cellular proliferations, and fibrosis lead to narrowing or closure of the vessel lumen.

584
01:14:20,804 --> 01:14:28,267
These vascular changes create pulmonary hypertension and a resultant reversal of the intracardiac shunt (B).

585
01:14:28,267 --> 01:14:37,179
With sustained pulmonary hypertension, extensive atherosclerosis and calcification often develop in the large pulmonary arteries.

586
01:14:37,179 --> 01:14:47,442
Although a VSD is shown here, Eisenmenger syndrome may also develop in association with a large atrial septal defect or persistent ductus arteriosus.

587
01:14:47,442 --> 01:14:56,917
Pregnant women with Eisenmenger syndrome tolerate hypotension poorly, and death usually is caused by right ventricular failure with cardiogenic shock.

588
01:14:56,917 --> 01:15:05,392
In a review of 73 pregnancies, Weiss and associates (1998) cited a 36-percent maternal death rate.

589
01:15:05,392 --> 01:15:12,517
Three of 26 deaths were antepartum, and the remainder of women died intrapartum or within a month of delivery.

590
01:15:12,517 --> 01:15:20,592
In another study of 13 gravidas, one mother died 17 days after delivery, and there were five perinatal deaths .

591
01:15:20,592 --> 01:15:25,654
Last, in a series of 11 pregnancies in China, four mothers died .

592
01:15:25,654 --> 01:15:42,942
Given such poor outcomes for both mother and fetus, Eisenmenger syndrome is considered to be an absolute contraindication to pregnancy (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019; Foeller, 2018; Meng, 2017).

593
01:15:42,942 --> 01:15:45,304
Pulmonary Hypertension.

594
01:15:45,304 --> 01:15:51,304
Normal resting mean pulmonary artery pressure is 12 to 16 mm Hg.

595
01:15:51,304 --> 01:16:00,567
Most define pulmonary hypertension in nonpregnant individuals as a resting mean pulmonary pressure >25 mm Hg .

596
01:16:00,567 --> 01:16:15,642
Pulmonary vascular resistance in late normal pregnancy approximates 80 dyne/sec/cm−5, which is 34-percent less than the nonpregnant value of 120 dyne/sec/cm−5 .

597
01:16:15,642 --> 01:16:26,354
Physiologically, pregnancy is associated increased cardiac output, and in healthy gravidas, pulmonary vascular resistance decreases to help accommodate this .

598
01:16:26,354 --> 01:16:34,742
With pulmonary hypertension, the stiff pulmonary vasculature does not allow the normal fall in pulmonary vascular resistance.

599
01:16:34,742 --> 01:16:43,854
Thus, the normal increased cardiac output actually leads to further pulmonary artery pressure elevation and then eventually to right heart failure.

600
01:16:43,854 --> 01:16:56,804
With failure, the interventricular septum bulges leftward to impair left ventricular diastolic filling, which compromises cardiac output (Gei, 2014; Pieper, 2011).

601
01:16:56,804 --> 01:17:08,167
The current clinical classification system, shown in Table 52-8, contains five groups of disorders that cause pulmonary hypertension (Galiè, 2016).

602
01:17:08,167 --> 01:17:15,904
Important prognostic and therapeutic distinctions separate group 1 pulmonary arterial hypertension and the other groups.

603
01:17:15,904 --> 01:17:23,654
Group 1, which is more common in nonpregnant women, indicates that a specific disease affects pulmonary arterioles.

604
01:17:23,654 --> 01:17:33,367
It includes idiopathic or primary pulmonary arterial hypertension as well as those cases secondary to a known cause such as connective tissue disease.

605
01:17:33,367 --> 01:17:43,004
For example, approximately a third of women with scleroderma and 10 percent with systemic lupus erythematosus have pulmonary arterial hypertension .

606
01:17:43,004 --> 01:17:45,404
Other causes in young women

607
01:17:45,556 --> 01:17:53,131
are human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, sickle-cell disease, and thyrotoxicosis.

608
01:17:53,081 --> 01:17:55,543
Table 52-8.

609
01:17:55,543 --> 01:18:07,843
Comprehensive Clinical Classification of Pulmonary Hypertension of the European Society of Cardiology and the European Respiratory Society In pregnant women, group 2 disorders are the most common.

610
01:18:07,843 --> 01:18:16,193
These are secondary to pulmonary venous hypertension caused by left-sided atrial, ventricular, or valvular disorders.

611
01:18:16,193 --> 01:18:21,993
A typical example is mitral stenosis discussed earlier (p. 924).

612
01:18:21,993 --> 01:18:28,218
In contrast, groups 3 through 5 are seen infrequently in young otherwise healthy women.

613
01:18:28,218 --> 01:18:31,518
# Diagnosis and Prognosis.

614
01:18:31,518 --> 01:18:36,493
Symptoms may be vague, and dyspnea with exertion is the most frequent.

615
01:18:36,493 --> 01:18:42,418
With group 2 disorders, orthopnea and nocturnal dyspnea are also usually present.

616
01:18:42,418 --> 01:18:49,243
Angina and syncope occur when right ventricular output is fixed, and they suggest advanced disease.

617
01:18:49,243 --> 01:18:56,268
Chest radiography often shows enlarged pulmonary hilar arteries and attenuated peripheral markings.

618
01:18:56,268 --> 01:19:00,318
It may also disclose parenchymal causes of hypertension.

619
01:19:00,318 --> 01:19:10,131
Noninvasive echocardiography can provide an estimate of pulmonary artery pressures, although cardiac catheterization remains the standard for measurement.

620
01:19:10,131 --> 01:19:27,006
In studies of pregnant women who underwent both echocardiography and cardiac catheterization, pulmonary artery pressures were significantly overestimated by echocardiography in approximately a third of cases (Herrera, 2020; Wylie, 2007).

621
01:19:27,006 --> 01:19:34,331
Regardless of the etiology, the final common pathway of pulmonary hypertension is right heart failure and death.

622
01:19:34,331 --> 01:19:39,106
The average survival length after diagnosis is <4 years .

623
01:19:39,106 --> 01:19:45,631
That said, longevity depends on the severity and cause of pulmonary hypertension at discovery.

624
01:19:45,631 --> 01:19:52,593
As discussed later, some disorders respond to medical interventions, which may improve quality of life.

625
01:19:52,593 --> 01:19:56,856
Preconceptional and contraceptive counseling are imperative .

626
01:19:56,856 --> 01:19:59,106
# Pregnancy.

627
01:19:59,106 --> 01:20:07,968
The maternal mortality rate is appreciable in affected women, and this is especially so with idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension .

628
01:20:07,968 --> 01:20:14,818
In the past, the ability to accurately identify causes and assess disease severity were often poor.

629
01:20:14,818 --> 01:20:27,318
Thus, although most severe cases of idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension had the worst prognosis, it was erroneously assumed that all types of pulmonary hypertension were equally dangerous.

630
01:20:27,318 --> 01:20:34,281
With widespread use of echocardiography, less-severe lesions with a better prognosis are now discernible.

631
01:20:34,281 --> 01:20:39,031
The maternal mortality rate for pulmonary hypertension has improved.

632
01:20:39,031 --> 01:20:50,356
In one study, it was 25 percent during the decade ending in 2007 compared with 38 percent for the decade ending in 1996 (Bédard, 2009).

633
01:20:50,356 --> 01:20:55,843
Importantly, almost 80 percent of the deaths were during the first month postpartum.

634
01:20:55,843 --> 01:21:04,493
Meng and associates (2017) reported mortality rates of 23 percent with group 1 and 5 percent with the other groups.

635
01:21:04,493 --> 01:21:20,743
Mortality risk correlates positively with advancing pulmonary hypertension severity, which is characterized by Eisenmenger syndrome, severe hypertension, and higher NYHA class (Keepanasseril, 2019; Sun, 2018).

636
01:21:20,743 --> 01:21:29,656
In an audit of 47 pregnancies in women with pulmonary hypertension at Parkland Hospital, the maternal mortality rate was 9 percent .

637
01:21:29,656 --> 01:21:33,681
Of four deaths, three women had severe hypertension.

638
01:21:33,681 --> 01:21:41,331
A striking example of right ventricular hypertrophy in one of these women who died is shown in Figure 52-6.

639
01:21:41,331 --> 01:21:44,006
Figure 52-6.

640
01:21:44,006 --> 01:21:52,506
Cross-sectional display of right cardiac dysmorphology in a pregnant woman who died from undiagnosed primary pulmonary hypertension.

641
01:21:52,506 --> 01:21:58,306
There is prominent dilation of the right ventricle with right ventricular hypertrophy (arrow).

642
01:21:58,306 --> 01:22:01,993
Pregnancy is contraindicated with severe disease.

643
01:22:01,993 --> 01:22:09,156
This is especially true in women with pulmonary arterial changes, which develop in most group 1 cases.

644
01:22:09,156 --> 01:22:15,456
With milder disease from other causes—group 2 being the most common—the prognosis is better.

645
01:22:15,456 --> 01:22:29,943
With the more frequent use of echocardiography and pulmonary artery catheterization in young women with heart disease, we have identified women with mild to moderate pulmonary hypertension who tolerate pregnancy, labor, and delivery well .

646
01:22:29,943 --> 01:22:32,168
# Management.

647
01:22:32,168 --> 01:22:35,731
Treatment of symptomatic pregnant women includes

648
01:22:35,884 --> 01:22:40,509
limiting activity and avoiding supine position later in gestation.

649
01:22:40,459 --> 01:22:47,634
Diuretics, supplemental oxygen, and pulmonary vasodilator drugs are standard therapy for symptoms.

650
01:22:47,634 --> 01:22:50,696
Some recommend anticoagulation.

651
01:22:50,696 --> 01:23:00,896
Several reports describe the successful use of intravenous pulmonary artery vasodilators (Foeller, 2018; Franco, 2019).

652
01:23:00,896 --> 01:23:12,221
Prostacyclin analogues that can be administered parenterally include epoprostenol (Flolan) and treprostinil (Remodulin), whereas iloprost (Ventavis) is inhaled.

653
01:23:12,221 --> 01:23:14,821
Each has been used in pregnancy.

654
01:23:14,821 --> 01:23:21,871
Inhaled nitric oxide is an option that has been employed in cases of acute cardiopulmonary decompensation.

655
01:23:21,871 --> 01:23:38,259
As reviewed by Običan and Cleary (2014), phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors, such as sildenafil (Viagra), cause vasodilation of both the pulmonary and systemic vascular beds and have an inotropic effect on the hypertrophic right ventricle.

656
01:23:38,259 --> 01:23:42,196
This also has been used to advantage during pregnancy .

657
01:23:42,196 --> 01:23:53,409
Bosentan—an endothelinreceptor antagonist, and riociguat—a soluble guanylate cyclase stimulator, are teratogenic in mice and contraindicated in pregnancy .

658
01:23:53,409 --> 01:24:00,409
During labor and delivery, supplemental oxygen is given to maintain >90 percent saturation.

659
01:24:00,409 --> 01:24:06,284
These women are at greatest risk when venous return and right ventricular filling are diminished.

660
01:24:06,284 --> 01:24:20,209
To avoid hypotension, assiduous attention is given to epidural analgesia induction, fluid therapy, and blood loss prevention and treatment at delivery (Martin, 2019; Meng, 2017).

661
01:24:20,209 --> 01:24:22,484
Cardiomyopathies.

662
01:24:22,484 --> 01:24:31,984
The American Heart Association defines these as a heterogeneous group of myocardial diseases associated with mechanical and/or electrical dysfunction .

663
01:24:31,984 --> 01:24:37,509
Affected women usually have inappropriate ventricular hypertrophy or dilation.

664
01:24:37,509 --> 01:24:42,984
Cardiomyopathies stem from varied causes, and the most frequent is genetic.

665
01:24:42,984 --> 01:24:50,246
Of the two major divisions, primary cardiomyopathies are solely or predominantly confined to the heart muscle.

666
01:24:50,246 --> 01:24:58,121
Examples are hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, dilated cardiomyopathies, and peripartum cardiomyopathy.

667
01:24:58,121 --> 01:25:06,134
Secondary cardiomyopathies result from generalized systemic disorders that produce pathological myocardial involvement.

668
01:25:06,134 --> 01:25:14,484
Diabetes, systemic lupus erythematosus, chronic hypertension, and thyroid disorders are representative conditions.

669
01:25:14,484 --> 01:25:17,934
# Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy.

670
01:25:17,934 --> 01:25:22,384
This disorder affects approximately 1 in 500 adults .

671
01:25:22,384 --> 01:25:36,346
Characterized by cardiac hypertrophy, myocyte disarray, and interstitial fibrosis, the condition in up to 60 percent of affected patients is caused by mutations in genes that encode cardiac sarcomere proteins.

672
01:25:36,346 --> 01:25:46,334
In such cases, inheritance is autosomal dominant, and genetic screening is complex (Cirino, 2019; Elliott, 2014).

673
01:25:46,334 --> 01:25:55,621
Other genetic and nongenetic etiologies underlie 5 to 10 percent of cases, and the cause is unknown in approximately 25 percent.

674
01:25:55,621 --> 01:26:05,871
The resulting myocardial muscle abnormality is typified by left ventricular myocardial hypertrophy with a pressure gradient against left ventricular outflow.

675
01:26:05,871 --> 01:26:16,096
Diagnosis is established by echocardiographic identification of a hypertrophied and nondilated left ventricle in the absence of other cardiovascular conditions.

676
01:26:16,096 --> 01:26:24,984
Most affected women are asymptomatic, but dyspnea, anginal or atypical chest pain, syncope, and arrhythmias may develop.

677
01:26:24,984 --> 01:26:30,734
Complex arrhythmias may progress to sudden death, which is the most frequent cause of death.

678
01:26:30,734 --> 01:26:37,121
Asymptomatic patients with runs of ventricular tachycardia are especially prone to sudden death.

679
01:26:37,121 --> 01:26:40,371
Symptoms usually worsen with exercise.

680
01:26:40,371 --> 01:26:48,321
Studies that include more than 700 pregnancies in 500 women indicate an overall relatively good prognosis .

681
01:26:48,321 --> 01:27:00,046
In a systematic review with 237 women with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy who had a combined 408 pregnancies, the maternal mortality rate was 0.5 percent .

682
01:27:00,046 --> 01:27:07,709
Worsening of symptoms or other complications developed in 29 percent, and 26 percent delivered preterm.

683
01:27:07,709 --> 01:27:13,171
Management is similar to that for aortic stenosis (p. 926).

684
01:27:13,171 --> 01:27:18,909
Controlling heart rate and avoiding preload and afterload reduction are therapy basics.

685
01:27:18,909 --> 01:27:22,846
Strenuous exercise is prohibited during pregnancy.

686
01:27:22,846 --> 01:27:29,096
Abrupt positional changes are avoided to prevent reflex vasodilation and decreased preload.

687
01:27:29,096 --> 01:27:30,909
If symptoms

688
01:27:31,060 --> 01:27:37,397
develop, especially angina, β-adrenergic or calcium-channel blocking drugs are given.

689
01:27:37,347 --> 01:27:44,535
Drugs that evoke diuresis or reduce vascular resistance are generally not used because they decrease preload.

690
01:27:44,535 --> 01:27:48,585
But if these are necessary, women should be closely monitored.

691
01:27:48,585 --> 01:27:52,922
The delivery route is determined by obstetrical indications.

692
01:27:52,922 --> 01:27:59,010
Choice of anesthesia is controversial, and some consider general anesthesia the safest.

693
01:27:59,010 --> 01:28:07,860
Regional analgesia can be used with carefully titrated, adequate intravascular volume to maintain left ventricular filling pressure.

694
01:28:07,860 --> 01:28:11,922
Neonates rarely demonstrate inherited lesions at birth.

695
01:28:11,922 --> 01:28:15,160
# Dilated Cardiomyopathy.

696
01:28:15,160 --> 01:28:28,160
This is characterized by left and/or right ventricular enlargement and reduced systolic function in the absence of coronary, valvular, congenital, or systemic disease known to cause myocardial dysfunction.

697
01:28:28,160 --> 01:28:38,010
Although there are many known inherited and acquired causes of dilated cardiomyopathy, the etiology remains undefined in approximately half of cases .

698
01:28:38,010 --> 01:28:43,747
Some result from viral infections, including myocarditis and HIV.

699
01:28:43,747 --> 01:28:54,860
Other causes, which are potentially reversible, include alcoholism, cocaine abuse, coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19), and thyroid disease.

700
01:28:54,860 --> 01:29:09,472
In affected gravidas, the rate of major adverse cardiovascular events in pregnancy ranges from 25 to 40 percent (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019; Grewal, 2009).

701
01:29:09,472 --> 01:29:21,047
Heart failure and arrhythmias are the most common, and women with preexisting moderate or severe left ventricular dysfunction or NYHA functional class III or IV are at greatest risk.

702
01:29:21,047 --> 01:29:28,660
Dilated cardiomyopathy is managed with therapy for standard heart failure and for the specific underlying etiology .

703
01:29:28,660 --> 01:29:32,047
# Peripartum Cardiomyopathy.

704
01:29:32,047 --> 01:29:40,297
This disorder is similar to other forms of nonischemic dilated cardiomyopathy except for its unique relationship with pregnancy.

705
01:29:40,297 --> 01:29:52,997
Peripartum cardiomyopathy shares a genetic predisposition with both familial and sporadic idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy (Cunningham, 2019; Ware, 2016).

706
01:29:52,997 --> 01:29:59,260
It is a diagnosis of exclusion following a concurrent evaluation for peripartum heart failure.

707
01:29:59,260 --> 01:30:09,210
Although the term peripartum cardiomyopathy has been used widely, until recently, little evidence supported a unique pregnancy-induced cardiomyopathy.

708
01:30:09,210 --> 01:30:26,610
Pearson (2000) reported findings of a workshop of the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute that established the following diagnostic criteria: Development of cardiac failure in the last month of pregnancy or within 5 months after delivery , Figure 52-7.

709
01:30:26,610 --> 01:30:30,947
Peripartum cardiomyopathy with mild pulmonary edema.

710
01:30:30,947 --> 01:30:39,960
Chest radiograph of a woman with an abnormally enlarged heart and mild perihilar opacification consistent with dilated cardiomyopathy.

711
01:30:39,960 --> 01:30:54,347
Absence of an identifiable cause for the cardiac failure, • Absence of recognizable heart disease prior to the last month of pregnancy, and • Left ventricular systolic dysfunction demonstrated by classic echocardiographic criteria.

712
01:30:54,347 --> 01:31:00,797
These include depressed ejection fraction or fractional shortening along with a dilated left ventricle.

713
01:31:00,797 --> 01:31:09,822
The incidence of peripartum cardiomyopathy varies considerably and depends on the population studied and the diligence of the search for a cause.

714
01:31:09,822 --> 01:31:19,572
The average frequency in the United States is 1 per 1000 to 4000 births (Cruz, 2018; Cunningham, 2019).

715
01:31:19,572 --> 01:31:39,060
The etiology of peripartum cardiomyopathy remains unknown, and proposed causes include viral myocarditis, abnormal immune response to pregnancy, aberrant response to the greater hemodynamic burden of pregnancy, hormonal interactions, malnutrition, inflammation, and apoptosis.

716
01:31:39,060 --> 01:31:47,935
The current theory regarding pathophysiology is that of a “two-hit hypothesis” (Cruz, 2018; Ricke-Hoch, 2020).

717
01:31:47,935 --> 01:32:00,035
In this regard, peripartum cardiomyopathy affects genetically susceptible women who have one of several cardiac gene mutations to include TTNC1, TTN, and STAT3.

718
01:32:00,035 --> 01:32:06,722
Pregnancy at term is further characterized by prodigious secretion of prolactin by the maternal pituitary.

719
01:32:06,722 --> 01:32:16,360
At the same time, the placenta secretes high levels of the antiangiogenic molecule soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase (sFlt-1).

720
01:32:16,360 --> 01:32:18,772
Although a number of putative

721
01:32:18,940 --> 01:32:30,152
triggering events have been hypothesized, a 16-kDa prolactin fragment—vasoinhibin—acts to cause myocardial damage with clinically apparent ventricular dysfunction.

722
01:32:30,102 --> 01:32:39,102
This is made worse by high levels of sFlt-1, which is superabundant in women with preeclampsia, multifetal pregnancy, or both.

723
01:32:39,102 --> 01:32:47,352
Bromocriptine therapy has been evaluated because it inhibits prolactin secretion, and preliminary studies support its use .

724
01:32:47,352 --> 01:32:51,902
For the same reason, it is also reasonable to proscribe breastfeeding.

725
01:32:51,902 --> 01:33:02,590
Hypertensive disorders frequently coexist with peripartum cardiomyopathy, and another proposed mechanism links peripartum cardiomyopathy to preeclampsia .

726
01:33:02,590 --> 01:33:11,390
Antiangiogenic factors are known to be associated with preeclampsia and can induce peripartum cardiomyopathy in susceptible mice.

727
01:33:11,390 --> 01:33:21,615
Thus, cardiomyopathy may be precipitated by antiangiogenic factors in a genetically predisposed host because of insufficient proangiogenic factors.

728
01:33:21,615 --> 01:33:31,915
Ntusi and coworkers (2015) analyzed the clinical features of women with peripartum cardiomyopathy compared with those with hypertensive heart failure.

729
01:33:31,915 --> 01:33:43,152
All women with peripartum cardiomyopathy became symptomatic in the postpartum period, whereas 85 percent of women with hypertensive heart failure developed symptoms antepartum.

730
01:33:43,152 --> 01:33:51,527
Peripartum cardiomyopathy was significantly linked with twin gestation, smoking, and echocardiographic abnormalities.

731
01:33:51,527 --> 01:34:03,902
In contrast, hypertensive heart failure patients more often had a family history of hypertension, hypertension and preeclampsia in a prior pregnancy, and left ventricular hypertrophy.

732
01:34:03,902 --> 01:34:12,727
Management Management of peripartum cardiomyopathy is the same as that of heart failure, and described earlier (p. 922).

733
01:34:12,727 --> 01:34:19,640
Therapy is aimed at volume overload, afterload reduction, rhythm control, and inotropic support .

734
01:34:19,640 --> 01:34:24,702
In addition, β-blocker therapy is used to decrease mortality rates.

735
01:34:24,702 --> 01:34:39,627
Anticoagulation with LMWH is considered when the ejection fraction reaches 30 to 35 percent because of the increased risk for left ventricular thrombi (Bauersachs, 2016; Bozkurt, 2016).

736
01:34:39,627 --> 01:34:48,377
In one pilot study, bromocriptine resulted in higher rates of left ventricular recovery at 6 months and lower mortality rates .

737
01:34:48,377 --> 01:34:58,265
A larger study of 115 women showed no difference in full restoration of left ventricular function between those treated and untreated with bromocriptine .

738
01:34:58,265 --> 01:35:07,665
Another investigation of 63 women showed no difference in left ventricular function improvement with short-term versus long-term bromocriptine therapy .

739
01:35:07,665 --> 01:35:13,290
The use of bromocriptine as adjuvant therapy is considered experimental at this time.

740
01:35:13,290 --> 01:35:22,727
Prognosis Approximately half of women suffering from peripartum cardiomyopathy recover baseline ventricular function within 6 months of delivery.

741
01:35:22,727 --> 01:35:25,615
This rate is lower in obese women .

742
01:35:25,615 --> 01:35:38,215
In a group of 100 women with newly diagnosed peripartum cardiomyopathy, 72 percent had a left ventricular ejection fraction ≥50 percent at 1 year postpartum .

743
01:35:38,215 --> 01:35:45,702
Recovery to this level occurred in almost 90 percent of women whose baseline ejection fraction was at least 30 percent.

744
01:35:45,702 --> 01:35:53,290
This is compared with <40 percent in women whose baseline ventricular ejection fraction was <30 percent.

745
01:35:53,290 --> 01:35:59,115
Recovery was also related to the baseline left ventricular enddiastolic diameter.

746
01:35:59,115 --> 01:36:03,390
Event-free survival at 1 year occurred in 93 percent.

747
01:36:03,390 --> 01:36:13,465
Six women experienced nine major events that included four deaths, four left ventricular assist device implantations, and one heart transplantation.

748
01:36:13,465 --> 01:36:32,290
Li and colleagues (2016) also found that a baseline left ventricular ejection fraction <34 percent and a BNP level >1860 pg/mL were associated with an approximately threefold greater risk of persistent left ventricular systolic dysfunction.

749
01:36:32,290 --> 01:36:38,802
The mortality rate approaches 5 to 10 percent at 1 year in women with persistent cardiac failure .

750
01:36:38,802 --> 01:36:46,877
Long-term follow-up was reported in 28 women with a median surveillance of 91 months (Ersbøll, 2018).

751
01:36:46,877 --> 01:36:57,815
Although most women were asymptomatic, compared with controls, they had lower ejection fractions, less maximal exercise capacity, and subtle diastolic dysfunction.

752
01:36:57,815 --> 01:37:02,902
Subsequent Pregnancy From the largest studies on the topic, approximately

753
01:37:03,052 --> 01:37:13,764
a third of women with a history of peripartum cardiomyopathy will suffer relapse with worsening of symptoms and deterioration of left ventricular function during another pregnancy .

754
01:37:13,714 --> 01:37:24,339
The risk of relapse in women with persistent left ventricular dysfunction is substantially higher than in those who have recovered normal ventricular function before a subsequent pregnancy .

755
01:37:24,339 --> 01:37:36,614
However, normalization of left ventricular function does not guarantee an uncomplicated pregnancy, because approximately 20 percent of these women are at risk for deterioration in that function .

756
01:37:36,614 --> 01:37:39,939
# Other Cardiomyopathy Types.

757
01:37:39,939 --> 01:37:51,452
Arrhythmogenic right ventricular dysplasia is a unique cardiomyopathy defined histologically by progressive replacement of right ventricular myocardium with adipose and fibrous tissue.

758
01:37:51,452 --> 01:38:03,189
It has an estimated population prevalence of 1 in 5000, predisposes to ventricular tachyarrhythmias, and is a cause of sudden death, particularly in younger individuals .

759
01:38:03,189 --> 01:38:11,827
Studies of pregnancies in affected women reported symptoms, including heart failure, in 18 to 33 percent of pregnancies .

760
01:38:11,827 --> 01:38:18,827
Based on their systematic review, Krul and coworkers (2011) counsel against pregnancy.

761
01:38:18,827 --> 01:38:23,314
Restrictive cardiomyopathy is probably the least common type.

762
01:38:23,314 --> 01:38:35,614
This inherited cardiomyopathy is characterized by a ventricular filling pattern in which worsening myocardial stiffness raises ventricular pressure precipitously and allows only a small filling volume .

763
01:38:35,614 --> 01:38:41,752
Pregnancy is not advised because of the severe clinical course and poor prognosis in general.

764
01:38:41,752 --> 01:38:48,577
Takotsubo cardiomyopathy is a rare form of acute reversible left ventricular apical wall ballooning .

765
01:38:48,577 --> 01:38:55,302
It is considered to be “stress induced” and appears to be more common with preeclampsia and cesarean delivery.

766
01:38:55,302 --> 01:38:58,727
Myocardial infarction must be excluded .

767
01:38:58,727 --> 01:39:01,389
Infective Endocarditis.

768
01:39:01,389 --> 01:39:11,977
Persons at greatest risk for endocarditis are those with congenital heart lesions, intravenous drug use, degenerative valve disease, and intracardiac devices .

769
01:39:11,977 --> 01:39:20,927
Subacute bacterial endocarditis usually stems from a low-virulence bacterial infection superimposed on an underlying structural lesion.

770
01:39:20,927 --> 01:39:24,127
These are usually native valve infections.

771
01:39:24,127 --> 01:39:33,239
Organisms that cause indolent endocarditis are most often viridans-group streptococci or Staphylococcus or Enterococcus species.

772
01:39:33,239 --> 01:39:40,827
Among intravenous drug abusers and those with catheter-related infections, Staphylococcus aureus predominates.

773
01:39:40,827 --> 01:39:46,664
With prosthetic valve infections, Staphylococcus epidermidis is a frequent cause.

774
01:39:46,664 --> 01:39:53,439
Streptococcus pneumoniae and Neisseria gonorrhoeae may occasionally cause acute, fulminating disease.

775
01:39:53,439 --> 01:40:02,839
Others have reported other Neisseria species, group B streptococcus, and Escherichia coli endocarditis during pregnancy or peripartum.

776
01:40:02,839 --> 01:40:05,864
# Diagnosis and Management.

777
01:40:05,864 --> 01:40:10,964
Infective endocarditis symptoms vary and often develop insidiously.

778
01:40:10,964 --> 01:40:23,039
Fever, often with chills, is seen in 80 to 90 percent of cases; a murmur is heard in up to 85 percent; and anorexia, fatigue, and other constitutional symptoms are common .

779
01:40:23,039 --> 01:40:35,714
Clinical clues are anemia, proteinuria, and manifestations of embolic lesions that include petechiae, focal neurological changes, chest or abdominal pain, and extremity ischemia.

780
01:40:35,714 --> 01:40:39,077
In some cases, heart failure develops.

781
01:40:39,077 --> 01:40:46,652
Symptoms may persist for several weeks before the diagnosis is found, and a high index of suspicion is necessary.

782
01:40:46,652 --> 01:40:55,327
Diagnosis is made using the Duke-Li classification, which combines microbiology and imaging criteria with five minor criteria .

783
01:40:55,327 --> 01:41:03,377
Echocardiography may be diagnostic, but lesions <2 mm or those on the tricuspid valve may be missed.

784
01:41:03,377 --> 01:41:09,252
If uncertain, transesophageal echocardiography is accurate and informative.

785
01:41:09,252 --> 01:41:15,264
Importantly, a negative echocardiographic study does not exclude endocarditis.

786
01:41:15,264 --> 01:41:24,189
Treatment is primarily medical, and ascertainment of the infecting organism and its sensitivities is imperative for antimicrobial selection.

787
01:41:24,189 --> 01:41:35,427
Guidelines for appropriate antibiotic treatment are published by professional societies and updated regularly (Habib, 2015; Karchmer, 2018).

788
01:41:35,427 --> 01:41:42,864
Recalcitrant bacteremia and heart failure due to valvular dysfunction are a few reasons for which persistent infection

789
01:41:43,036 --> 01:41:45,773
may require valve replacement .

790
01:41:45,723 --> 01:41:47,973
# Pregnancy.

791
01:41:47,973 --> 01:41:53,273
Infective endocarditis is uncommon during pregnancy and the puerperium.

792
01:41:53,273 --> 01:42:01,198
In an earlier period, the incidence of endocarditis at Parkland Hospital approximated 1 in 16,000 births .

793
01:42:01,198 --> 01:42:07,148
With the current opioid use disorder epidemic, endocarditis may become more frequent .

794
01:42:07,148 --> 01:42:17,123
Associated maternal and fetal mortality rates are from 25 to 35 percent (Dagher, 2021; Habib, 2015).

795
01:42:17,123 --> 01:42:27,523
In one review, risk factors were intravenous drug use (14 percent), congenital heart disease (12 percent), and rheumatic heart disease (12 percent).

796
01:42:27,523 --> 01:42:35,586
The most common pathogens were streptococcal (43 percent) and staphylococcal (26 percent) species.

797
01:42:35,586 --> 01:42:40,911
Among 51 pregnancies, the maternal mortality rate was 11 percent .

798
01:42:40,911 --> 01:42:44,298
# Endocarditis Prophylaxis.

799
01:42:44,298 --> 01:42:52,436
For years, patients with any heart valve problem were given periprocedural antibiotics for endocarditis prophylaxis.

800
01:42:52,436 --> 01:42:56,561
Currently, however, recommendations are more stringent.

801
01:42:56,561 --> 01:43:19,173
The American Heart Association recommends prophylaxis for dental procedures in those with: (1) a prosthetic valve or prosthetic material used in a valve repair, (2) prior endocarditis, (3) unrepaired cyanotic heart defect or repaired lesion with residual defect at prosthetic sites, and (4) valvulopathy after heart transplantation .

802
01:43:19,173 --> 01:43:34,261
In the absence of pelvic infection, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018b) does not recommend endocarditis prophylaxis for either vaginal or cesarean delivery, except with the lesions cited above.

803
01:43:34,261 --> 01:43:42,336
Pregnant women at highest risk for endocarditis are those with cyanotic cardiac disease, prosthetic valves, or both.

804
01:43:42,336 --> 01:43:54,661
Table 52-9 shows prophylactic regimens for women not already receiving intrapartum antimicrobial therapy for another indication that would also provide coverage against endocarditis.

805
01:43:54,661 --> 01:44:01,361
These are administered as close to 30 to 60 minutes before the anticipated delivery time as is feasible.

806
01:44:01,361 --> 01:44:03,998
Table 52-9.

807
01:44:03,998 --> 01:44:10,761
Single-Dose Antibiotic Prophylaxis for Infective Endocarditis in High-Risk Patients Arrhythmias.

808
01:44:10,761 --> 01:44:19,323
Both preexisting and new-onset cardiac arrhythmias are often encountered during pregnancy, labor, delivery, and the puerperium.

809
01:44:19,323 --> 01:44:24,361
They are usually benign and their incidence in pregnancy appears to be increasing .

810
01:44:24,361 --> 01:44:28,673
The mechanism(s) responsible for the higher incidence are not clear.

811
01:44:28,673 --> 01:44:33,473
From some studies, estradiol and progesterone are proarrhythmic.

812
01:44:33,473 --> 01:44:42,098
Estrogen augments the number of adrenergic receptors in the myocardium, and adrenergic responsiveness seems to be greater in pregnancy .

813
01:44:42,098 --> 01:44:50,136
Perhaps the normal but mild hypokalemia of pregnancy and/or the physiological rise in heart rate serves to induce arrhythmias.

814
01:44:50,136 --> 01:44:57,111
Alternatively, detection of arrhythmias may be greater because of the frequent visits in routine prenatal care.

815
01:44:57,111 --> 01:44:59,661
# Bradyarrhythmias.

816
01:44:59,661 --> 01:45:06,186
Slow heart rhythms, including complete heart block, are compatible with a successful pregnancy outcome .

817
01:45:06,186 --> 01:45:14,536
Some women with complete heart block have syncope during labor and delivery, and occasionally temporary cardiac pacing is necessary.

818
01:45:14,536 --> 01:45:21,898
In our experiences and from others, women with permanent artificial pacemakers usually tolerate pregnancy well .

819
01:45:21,898 --> 01:45:28,261
With fixed-rate devices, cardiac output apparently is increased by augmented stroke volume.

820
01:45:28,261 --> 01:45:34,598
Patients with pacemakers or other electrical implants require special precautions during surgery.

821
01:45:34,598 --> 01:45:41,936
Stray current may be interpreted as an intracardiac signal by the implanted device and lead to pacing changes.

822
01:45:41,936 --> 01:45:50,648
In addition, myocardial burns may result from conduction of electrosurgical current to the pacing electrode rather than to the grounding pad.

823
01:45:50,648 --> 01:46:16,248
With these devices, preventive steps include cardiology consultation; bipolar electrosurgery or Harmonic scalpel use rather than monopolar current; if needed, minimal monopolar settings; continuous cardiac and pulse oximetry monitoring; contingency plans for arrhythmias; and close proximity of active (electrosurgical pencil) and return (electrosurgical grounding pad) electrodes .

824
01:46:16,248 --> 01:46:19,723
# Supraventricular Tachycardias.

825
01:46:19,723 --> 01:46:27,373
The most common arrhythmia seen in reproductive-aged women is paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (SVT).

826
01:46:27,373 --> 01:46:30,273
The prevalence during pregnancy is

827
01:46:30,436 --> 01:46:40,173
24 cases per 100,000 hospital admissions, and approximately 20 percent will experience symptomatic exacerbations during pregnancy .

828
01:46:40,123 --> 01:46:51,523
Interestingly, the mean heart rate of pregnant women with paroxysmal SVT is 184 bpm compared with 166 bpm in nonpregnant affected women .

829
01:46:51,523 --> 01:47:00,861
In one study, approximately half of women with paroxysmal SVT had an initial onset during pregnancy (Bánhidy, 2015).

830
01:47:00,861 --> 01:47:12,561
Notably, maternal paroxysmal SVT was associated with a twofold higher risk of septal cardiac defects, particularly secundum atrial septal defects, in their offspring.

831
01:47:12,561 --> 01:47:26,473
For acute SVT treatment, vagal maneuvers, which include Valsalva maneuver, carotid sinus massage, bearing down, and immersion of the face in ice water, raise vagal tone and block the atrioventricular node.

832
01:47:26,473 --> 01:47:33,211
Intravenous adenosine is a short-acting endogenous nucleotide that also blocks AV nodal conduction.

833
01:47:33,211 --> 01:47:41,873
Our experiences are similar to those of others in that adenosine is safe and effective for cardioversion in hemodynamically stable gravidas .

834
01:47:41,873 --> 01:47:46,298
Transient fetal bradycardia has been described with adenosine .

835
01:47:46,298 --> 01:48:00,123
If pharmacological therapy is ineffective or contraindicated, the American College of Cardiology and the American Heart Association recommend synchronized cardioversion in pregnant women with hemodynamically unstable SVT .

836
01:48:00,123 --> 01:48:07,861
Although electrical cardioversion with standard energy settings is not contraindicated in pregnancy, vigilance is important.

837
01:48:07,861 --> 01:48:17,611
Barnes and colleagues (2002) described a case in which direct current cardioversion led to a sustained uterine contraction and fetal bradycardia.

838
01:48:17,611 --> 01:48:26,648
As an aside, pregnancy has no effect on the operation of implantable cardioverter-defibrillator devices (Boulé, 2014).

839
01:48:26,648 --> 01:48:36,648
If cardioversion fails or is unsafe because of concurrent thrombus, then long-term anticoagulation and heart rate control with medication are necessary .

840
01:48:36,648 --> 01:49:03,736
Other treatment options recommended by the American College of Cardiology and the American Heart Association include: • Intravenous metoprolol or propranolol when adenosine is ineffective or contraindicated, • Intravenous verapamil when adenosine and β-blocking agents are ineffective or contraindicated, • Intravenous procainamide, • Intravenous amiodarone for potentially life-threatening SVT and when other therapies are ineffective or contraindicated.

841
01:49:03,736 --> 01:49:09,561
Atrial fibrillation and atrial flutter rarely present for the first time during pregnancy.

842
01:49:09,561 --> 01:49:23,036
A new-onset atrial fibrillation should prompt a search for underlying etiologies that include cardiac anomalies, hyperthyroidism, pulmonary embolism, drug toxicity, and electrolyte disturbances .

843
01:49:23,036 --> 01:49:26,711
Major complications include embolic stroke.

844
01:49:26,711 --> 01:49:34,698
When associated with mitral stenosis, pulmonary edema may develop in later pregnancy if the ventricular rate is increased.

845
01:49:34,698 --> 01:49:39,298
Unstable patients are treated with cardioversion and rate control.

846
01:49:39,298 --> 01:49:48,136
Pregnancy may predispose otherwise asymptomatic women with WolffParkinson-White (WPW) syndrome to exhibit arrhythmias.

847
01:49:48,136 --> 01:49:58,961
In a study of women with asymptomatic or mildly symptomatic disease, half developed SVT for the first time, and the other half experienced an increase in their attack rate .

848
01:49:58,961 --> 01:50:03,623
In some patients, accessory pathway ablation may be indicated.

849
01:50:03,623 --> 01:50:08,573
Patients with Ebstein anomaly are prone to have WPW syndrome.

850
01:50:08,573 --> 01:50:13,311
Driver and associates (2015) have provided a review.

851
01:50:13,311 --> 01:50:16,348
# Ventricular Tachycardia.

852
01:50:16,348 --> 01:50:23,411
This form of arrhythmia is uncommon but potentially fatal in healthy young women without underlying heart disease.

853
01:50:23,411 --> 01:50:32,811
Brodsky and coworkers (1992) described seven pregnant women with new-onset ventricular tachycardia and reviewed 23 reports.

854
01:50:32,811 --> 01:50:37,061
Most of these women were not found to have structural heart disease.

855
01:50:37,061 --> 01:50:44,098
In 14 cases, tachycardia was precipitated by physical exercise or psychological stress.

856
01:50:44,098 --> 01:50:53,761
Abnormalities found included two cases of myocardial infarction, two of prolonged QT interval, and one of anesthesiaprovoked tachycardia.

857
01:50:53,761 --> 01:51:00,961
They concluded that pregnancy events precipitated the tachycardia and recommended β-blocking agents for control.

858
01:51:00,961 --> 01:51:09,736
As previously discussed (p. 934), arrhythmogenic right ventricular dysplasia will result occasionally in ventricular

859
01:51:09,892 --> 01:51:11,942
tachyarrhythmias.

860
01:51:11,892 --> 01:51:19,004
If unstable, emergency cardioversion is indicated, and standard adult energy settings are adequate .

861
01:51:19,004 --> 01:51:21,879
# Prolonged QT-Interval.

862
01:51:21,879 --> 01:51:26,517
The long QT syndrome is the most frequent inherited channelopathy .

863
01:51:26,517 --> 01:51:34,567
This conduction anomaly may predispose individuals to a potentially fatal ventricular arrhythmia known as torsades de pointes.

864
01:51:34,567 --> 01:51:48,629
Two studies comprised of 502 pregnant women with long QT syndrome both reported a significant rise in cardiac events postpartum but not during pregnancy (Rashba, 1998; Seth, 2007).

865
01:51:48,629 --> 01:51:53,829
The normal elevation in heart rate during pregnancy may be partially protective.

866
01:51:53,829 --> 01:52:06,017
Paradoxically, β-blocking agents—preferably propranolol—lower the risk of torsades de pointes in patients with long QT syndrome and should be continued throughout pregnancy and the puerperium .

867
01:52:06,017 --> 01:52:16,817
Importantly, many medications, including some used during pregnancy, such as the erythromycins and many antiemetics, may predispose to QT prolongation.

868
01:52:16,817 --> 01:52:23,279
For opioid-addicted women, the use of methadone to treat withdrawal symptoms may be problematic .

869
01:52:23,279 --> 01:52:31,279
Last, Cuneo and colleagues (2020) observed an eightfold risk for stillbirth in women with long QT syndrome .

870
01:52:31,279 --> 01:52:33,579
Diseases Of The Aorta.

871
01:52:33,579 --> 01:52:36,167
# Aortic Dissection.

872
01:52:36,167 --> 01:52:43,842
Marfan syndrome and coarctation are two aortic diseases that place the pregnant woman at greater risk for aortic dissection .

873
01:52:43,842 --> 01:52:50,729
Indeed, half of dissection cases in young women are related to pregnancy (O’Gara, 2004).

874
01:52:50,729 --> 01:53:01,842
Other risk factors are bicuspid aortic valve and Turner, Noonan, Loeys-Dietz, or Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (Cauldwell, 2019b; Russo, 2018).

875
01:53:01,842 --> 01:53:09,354
Pregnancy-related cardiac guidance for women with Turner syndrome is outlined in Chapter 3 (p. 37).

876
01:53:09,354 --> 01:53:19,817
Although the mechanism(s) involved are unclear, the initiating event is a tear in the intimal layer of the aorta, followed by hemorrhage into the media, and finally rupture.

877
01:53:19,817 --> 01:53:27,179
In most cases, aortic dissection presents with severe chest pain described as ripping, tearing, or stabbing.

878
01:53:27,179 --> 01:53:35,392
Diminution or loss of peripheral pulses coupled with a recently acquired aortic insufficiency murmur is an important physical finding.

879
01:53:35,392 --> 01:53:50,029
The differential diagnosis of aortic dissection in pregnancy includes myocardial infarction, pulmonary embolism, pneumothorax, aortic valve rupture, and obstetrical catastrophes such as placental abruption and uterine rupture.

880
01:53:50,029 --> 01:53:55,967
More than 90 percent of patients with aortic dissection have an abnormal chest radiograph.

881
01:53:55,967 --> 01:54:01,317
Aortic angiography is the most definitive method for diagnosis confirmation.

882
01:54:01,317 --> 01:54:09,392
However, sonography, computed tomography, and MR imaging are used more frequently depending on the clinical urgency.

883
01:54:09,392 --> 01:54:13,217
Initial medical treatment is given to lower blood pressure.

884
01:54:13,217 --> 01:54:19,817
Proximal dissections most often need to be resected, and the aortic valve replaced if necessary.

885
01:54:19,817 --> 01:54:24,592
Distal dissections are more complex, and many may be treated medically.

886
01:54:24,592 --> 01:54:36,792
Among nonpregnant patients with abdominal aortic aneurysms <5.5 cm, survival is not improved by immediate elective repair compared with surveillance and delayed repair.

887
01:54:36,792 --> 01:54:45,104
Karthikesalingam and associates (2016) suggest that the size threshold for aneurysm repair should be revisited.

888
01:54:45,104 --> 01:54:47,704
# Marfan Syndrome.

889
01:54:47,704 --> 01:54:57,642
This autosomal dominant connective tissue disorder has an incidence of 1 per 3000 to 5000 individuals and is without racial or ethnic predilection .

890
01:54:57,642 --> 01:55:08,542
As discussed in Chapter 62 (p. 1121), Marfan syndrome is caused by any of more than 1000 mutations in the fibrillin (FBN1) gene.

891
01:55:08,542 --> 01:55:15,629
It is characterized by generalized tissue weakness that can result in dangerous cardiovascular complications.

892
01:55:15,629 --> 01:55:21,804
All tissues are involved, and other frequent defects include joint laxity and scoliosis.

893
01:55:21,804 --> 01:55:32,079
Progressive aortic dilation causes aortic valve insufficiency, and infective endocarditis or mitral valve prolapse with insufficiency may be comorbid.

894
01:55:32,079 --> 01:55:37,392
Aortic dilation and dissecting aneurysm are the most serious abnormalities.

895
01:55:37,392 --> 01:55:43,629
Early death is due to either a dissecting aneurysm or to valvular insufficiency and heart failure.

896
01:55:43,629 --> 01:55:53,492
During pregnancy, the primary concern with Marfan syndrome is aortic dissection (Curry, 2014; Russo, 2017).

897
01:55:53,492 --> 01:55:59,267
A study using the Nationwide Inpatient Sample from 2003 to 2010

898
01:55:59,428 --> 01:56:04,428
described 339 deliveries in women with Marfan syndrome.

899
01:56:04,378 --> 01:56:10,065
There was one maternal death and six (1.8 percent) aortic dissections .

900
01:56:10,065 --> 01:56:19,665
From the United Kingdom, Cauldwell and colleagues (2019a) described 258 pregnancies in 151 affected women.

901
01:56:19,665 --> 01:56:24,665
Although no women died, 1.9 percent had an aortic dissection.

902
01:56:24,665 --> 01:56:32,240
The aortic root usually measures approximately 2 cm, and during normal pregnancy, it expands slightly.

903
01:56:32,240 --> 01:56:40,315
With Marfan syndrome, aortic root repair is recommended at diameters of 4.0 to 4.5 cm .

904
01:56:40,315 --> 01:56:56,465
The guidelines of the American College of Cardiology, the American Heart Association, and the American Association of Thoracic Surgeons advise prophylactic aortic repair in women considering pregnancy if the diameter of the ascending aorta exceeds 4 cm .

905
01:56:56,465 --> 01:57:05,240
The guidelines of the European Society of Cardiology advise repair of the aorta at diameters >4.5 cm .

906
01:57:05,240 --> 01:57:13,228
Surgical repair is also considered using a formula indexed to height because shorter patients have dissection at a smaller diameter .

907
01:57:13,228 --> 01:57:25,865
For pregnant women with known thoracic aortic root or ascending aortic dilation, monthly or bimonthly echocardiographic measurements of the ascending aortic dimensions are recommended to detect expansion .

908
01:57:25,865 --> 01:57:36,415
Prophylactic β-blocking agents have become standard for gravidas with Marfan syndrome because they reduce hemodynamic stress on the ascending aorta and slow the dilation rate.

909
01:57:36,415 --> 01:57:44,353
Ideally, pregnant women with aortic aneurysms are delivered at facilities in which cardiothoracic surgery is available.

910
01:57:44,353 --> 01:57:53,703
Vaginal delivery with regional analgesia and an assisted second stage seem safe for women with an aortic root diameter <4 cm.

911
01:57:53,703 --> 01:58:00,740
Cesarean delivery may be considered for values between 4.0 and 4.5 cm.

912
01:58:00,740 --> 01:58:12,853
When the aortic root measures >4.5 cm, elective cesarean delivery is recommended, and direct replacement of the proximal aorta with a prosthetic graft can be considered .

913
01:58:12,853 --> 01:58:22,678
Successful aortic root replacement during pregnancy has been described, but the surgery has also been associated with fetal hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy .

914
01:58:22,678 --> 01:58:37,828
Several case reports describe emergency cesarean deliveries in women with acute type A dissections that were repaired successfully at the time of delivery (Guo, 2011; Haas, 2011; Papatsonis, 2009).

915
01:58:37,828 --> 01:58:47,803
To evaluate obstetrical outcomes, investigators for one study of 63 women with Marfan syndrome analyzed their 142 pregnancies.

916
01:58:47,803 --> 01:58:58,778
Of 111 pregnancies progressing past 20 weeks’ gestation, 15 percent delivered preterm, and 5 percent had preterm prematurely ruptured membranes .

917
01:58:58,778 --> 01:59:06,165
There were eight perinatal deaths, and half of the neonatal survivors were subsequently diagnosed with Marfan syndrome.

918
01:59:06,165 --> 01:59:08,965
# Aortic Coarctation.

919
01:59:08,965 --> 01:59:17,415
In this relatively rare lesion, the aorta is abnormally narrowed and is often accompanied by abnormalities of other large arteries.

920
01:59:17,415 --> 01:59:25,265
A fourth of affected patients have a bicuspid aortic valve, and another 10 percent have cerebral artery aneurysms.

921
01:59:25,265 --> 01:59:32,153
Other associated lesions are persistent ductus arteriosus, septal defects, and Turner syndrome.

922
01:59:32,153 --> 01:59:44,165
The collateral circulation arising above the coarctation remodels and expands, often strikingly, to cause localized erosion of rib margins by hypertrophied intercostal arteries.

923
01:59:44,165 --> 01:59:51,540
Typical findings include hypertension in the upper extremities but normal or decreased pressures in the lower extremities.

924
01:59:51,540 --> 01:59:59,940
Some have described diagnosis during pregnancy using MR imaging (Sherer, 2002; Zwiers, 2006).

925
01:59:59,940 --> 02:00:10,903
Jimenez-Juan and associates (2014) found that aortic diameter measured by MR imaging and the risk of adverse events during pregnancy were inversely correlated.

926
02:00:10,903 --> 02:00:30,353
Of note, no adverse outcomes occurred if the minimum diameter at the coarctation exceeded 15 mm. Major complications with aortic coarctation include congestive heart failure after long-standing severe hypertension, bacterial endocarditis of an associated bicuspid aortic valve, and aortic rupture.

927
02:00:30,353 --> 02:00:38,140
Antihypertensive therapy using β-blocking drugs is usually required because hypertension may worsen in pregnancy.

928
02:00:38,140 --> 02:00:43,103
Aortic rupture is more likely late in pregnancy or early puerperium.

929
02:00:43,103 --> 02:00:47,915
Cerebral hemorrhage from circle of Willis aneurysms also may occur.

930
02:00:47,915 --> 02:00:49,728
According to

931
02:00:49,876 --> 02:00:55,163
the World Health Organization, severe coarctation should preclude pregnancy .

932
02:00:55,113 --> 02:01:08,213
In one study of outcomes from 188 pregnancies, a third of women had hypertension that was related to significant coarctation gradients, and one woman died from dissection at 36 weeks’ gestation .

933
02:01:08,213 --> 02:01:16,801
In 700 deliveries complicated with coarctation, hypertensive complications of pregnancy were increased three- to fourfold .

934
02:01:16,801 --> 02:01:32,226
Importantly, almost 5 percent of women with coarctation had an adverse cardiovascular outcome—maternal death, heart failure, arrhythmia, cerebrovascular or other embolic event—compared with only 0.3 percent of controls.

935
02:01:32,226 --> 02:01:40,126
Of women with coarctation, 41 percent underwent cesarean delivery compared with 26 percent of controls.

936
02:01:40,126 --> 02:01:47,451
Congestive heart failure demands vigorous efforts to improve cardiac function and may warrant pregnancy interruption.

937
02:01:47,451 --> 02:01:57,513
In this setting, some authors recommend that resection of the coarctation be undertaken to protect against the possibility of a dissecting aneurysm and aortic rupture.

938
02:01:57,513 --> 02:02:06,538
This poses significant perfusion risk, especially for the fetus, because all the arterial collaterals must be clamped for variable periods.

939
02:02:06,538 --> 02:02:08,776
Ischemic Heart Disease.

940
02:02:08,776 --> 02:02:20,026
Pregnant women with coronary artery disease commonly have the classic risk factors of family history, diabetes, smoking, hypertension, hyperlipidemia, and obesity .

941
02:02:20,026 --> 02:02:32,426
The rate of ischemic heart disease is estimated to be 10 cases per 100,000 pregnancy and postpartum hospitalizations (Smilowitz, 2018; Tripathi, 2019).

942
02:02:32,426 --> 02:02:42,801
Although relatively rare, the risk of acute myocardial infarction is approximately threefold higher in pregnant women compared with nonpregnant women of similar age .

943
02:02:42,801 --> 02:02:45,738
# Acute Coronary Syndrome.

944
02:02:45,738 --> 02:02:51,513
Myocardial infarction (MI) is the end result of an acute coronary syndrome.

945
02:02:51,513 --> 02:03:05,488
It can result from coronary artery atherosclerosis, dissection, embolism, spasm, or arteritis (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019; Cauldwell, 2020).

946
02:03:05,488 --> 02:03:11,463
The mortality rate with MI in pregnancy is higher compared with age-matched nonpregnant women.

947
02:03:11,463 --> 02:03:21,201
In a Nationwide Inpatient Sample study totaling 859 pregnancies complicated by acute MI, the death rate was 5.1 percent .

948
02:03:21,201 --> 02:03:30,588
Women who sustain an infarction <2 weeks before delivery are at especially high risk of death due to the greater myocardial demand of labor and delivery .

949
02:03:30,588 --> 02:03:39,251
In a systematic review of 150 cases, most women developed an acute MI during the third trimester or postpartum .

950
02:03:39,251 --> 02:03:45,076
Approximately three fourths presented with ST segment-elevation MI (STEMI).

951
02:03:45,076 --> 02:03:55,438
The leading mechanisms of acute infarction included spontaneous coronary dissection (43 percent) and atherosclerotic disease (27 percent).

952
02:03:55,438 --> 02:04:06,563
Significant complications included heart failure (38 percent), recurrent angina or infarction (19 percent), and ventricular arrhythmias (12 percent).

953
02:04:06,563 --> 02:04:12,026
The maternal and fetal mortality rates were 7 and 5 percent, respectively.

954
02:04:12,026 --> 02:04:29,563
Diagnosis of acute coronary syndrome during pregnancy does not differ from that in nonpregnant patients and is based on clinical presentation, characteristic ECG changes, and evidence of myocardial necrosis reflected by elevated serum high-sensitivity troponin I levels .

955
02:04:29,563 --> 02:04:37,851
Consider that troponin I levels are greater, however, in preeclamptic and hypertensive women compared with normotensive gravidas .

956
02:04:37,851 --> 02:04:47,538
With spontaneous coronary artery dissection, establishing the diagnosis requires an increased index of suspicion in the women presenting with chest pain .

957
02:04:47,538 --> 02:05:00,338
For this condition, coronary angiography is considered the diagnostic gold standard and should be expeditiously performed if acute coronary syndrome—either MI or unstable angina—is present .

958
02:05:00,338 --> 02:05:05,038
Treatment of acute MI is similar to that for nonpregnant patients .

959
02:05:05,038 --> 02:05:13,163
Several reports describe successful percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty and stent placement during pregnancy .

960
02:05:13,163 --> 02:05:20,451
Cardiopulmonary resuscitation may be required, as described in Chapter 50 (p. 897).

961
02:05:20,451 --> 02:05:30,238
If the infarct has healed sufficiently, cesarean delivery is reserved for obstetrical indications, and epidural analgesia is ideal for labor.

962
02:05:30,238 --> 02:05:34,138
# Pregnancy with Prior Ischemic Heart Disease.

963
02:05:34,138 --> 02:05:35,813
Ischemic

964
02:05:35,980 --> 02:05:46,405
heart disease is characteristically progressive, and because it is usually associated with hypertension or diabetes, pregnancy in most of these women seems inadvisable.

965
02:05:46,355 --> 02:05:59,042
In an earlier review of 30 pregnancies in women who had sustained an infarction remote from pregnancy, none of the women died, four had congestive heart failure, and four had worsening angina during pregnancy .

966
02:05:59,042 --> 02:06:08,192
Pombar and coworkers (1995) evaluated outcomes of women with diabetes-associated ischemic heart disease and infarction.

967
02:06:08,192 --> 02:06:13,267
Three had undergone coronary artery bypass grafting before pregnancy.

968
02:06:13,267 --> 02:06:17,030
Of 17 women, eight died during pregnancy.

969
02:06:17,030 --> 02:06:32,255
Certainly, pregnancy raises cardiac workload, and these investigators concluded that ventricular performance should be assessed using ventriculography, radionuclide studies, echocardiography, or coronary angiography before conception.

970
02:06:32,255 --> 02:06:37,830
Without significant ventricular dysfunction, pregnancy will likely be tolerated.

971
02:06:37,830 --> 02:06:43,855
For the woman who becomes pregnant before these studies are performed, echocardiography is done.

972
02:06:43,855 --> 02:06:52,205
Exercise tolerance testing may be indicated, and radionuclide ventriculography exposes the fetus to minimal radiation .

973
02:06:52,205 --> 02:06:55,042
Common Obstetrical Medications.

974
02:06:55,042 --> 02:07:01,692
Common medications used in pregnancy require special consideration in women with cardiac conditions.

975
02:07:01,692 --> 02:07:09,580
Terbutaline causes vasodilation and tachycardia, which can be dangerous in patients with mitral and aortic stenosis.

976
02:07:09,580 --> 02:07:21,892
Nifedipine for hypertension control or tocolysis results in hypotension that can have negative consequences for patients with aortic stenosis, pulmonary hypertension, and Eisenmenger syndrome.

977
02:07:21,892 --> 02:07:28,317
Postpartum hemorrhage should be managed aggressively, and methylergonovine generally is a safe choice.

978
02:07:28,317 --> 02:07:36,930
Prostaglandin F2α may cause pulmonary shunting and bronchospasm, which results in elevation of pulmonary artery pressure.

979
02:07:36,930 --> 02:07:44,717
Consideration is given to the vasodilatory effects of hydralazine in conditions such as hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.

980
02:07:44,717 --> 02:07:48,155
Hydralazine also causes tachycardia.

981
02:07:48,155 --> 02:07:53,742
The β-blocking agent labetalol is a safe choice unless there is ventricular failure.

982
02:07:53,742 --> 02:07:59,317
In general, oxytocin and magnesium sulfate have minimal cardiac effects.

